module engine where

-- =========================================================================
-- validation-entry.fenel — the Phase 4+4b TRUSTED VALIDATION ENTRY
-- (slice 3g; the second half of P4-D3).
--
-- THIS IS THE FIRST SLICE WHERE AUTHORITY IS REACHABLE. Slices 3a–3f were
-- inert by construction: the phase-4 static gate could say "no
-- judgment-reachable source references this vocabulary" and be believed, so a
-- mistake sat in a dormant module. From here a mistake ADMITS A SIGNATURE.
-- Every conservative instinct from those slices still binds, and the two that
-- bind hardest are: undecided fences, and a fence never moves in the admitting
-- direction.
--
-- WHY A NEW MODULE AND NOT A FLAG. 3f's result review supplied the sentence
-- this module is built against:
--
--   A public `vckLookup(carrier, arbitraryTable, id)` cannot establish
--   authenticity. Key isolation does not prove value authority.
--
-- 3f's preflight refuses a table whose keys are not in bijection with the
-- carrier's live identities, and refuses a table built against another body.
-- It cannot check that the arrow stored under occurrence `u` is an instance of
-- `u`'s target's scheme, because it ACCEPTS THE TABLE FROM ITS CALLER. The fix
-- is not a stronger check on a supplied table; it is that the authoritative
-- path never has one. `vteValidate` builds the carrier, builds the table, and
-- takes neither as an argument. `vckPreflightTable` / `vckLookup` stay
-- non-authoritative and unchanged, so the difference is one a static gate can
-- see rather than one a comment claims.
--
-- WHAT THIS SLICE DOES NOT DO, AND WHY IT IS NOT A CHOICE. P4-D3's second half
-- asks for the occurrence table to be built from a certificate-filtered
-- whole-body solve, allocating a fresh meta namespace per resolved use. That
-- cannot reuse HEAD's solver:
--
--   * the carrier walks the RAW stored body and keys occurrences by a TYPED
--     `[UsePathSeg]` — the S1 gate's finding is why, since re-lift is
--     non-injective and an identity minted from it names a site the runtime
--     never visits;
--   * `inferenceInfer` walks `Syntax` and threads a STRING path, and emits no
--     per-occurrence instantiation record at all;
--   * that string is not even occurrence-unique WITHIN `Syntax` — every
--     argument of a call shares one path, as do `do` elements and
--     corresponding fields across match arms.
--
-- So there is no correspondence to recover. What is forbidden is recovering
-- raw identity AFTER the existing provenance-free conversion; a NEW
-- provenance-bearing lowering that attaches an occurrence identity as it goes
-- would be sound, and the parent brief already anticipates one. That is a
-- substrate, not a wiring job, and it is slice 3i's.
--
-- THE GROUND-SCHEME CUT, AND THE FIVE CONDITIONS THAT MAKE IT SOUND. P4-D3's
-- own admission rule is that only a fully solved, residual-free, meta-free
-- monomorphic arrow may be installed for a use. A target whose published
-- scheme has zero quantified binders needs no instantiation — but zero binders
-- is ONLY "no prenex instantiation required". It is not exactness: a genuinely
-- dependent `$el` scheme is published with `quantified = []`, and an invalid
-- nominal application like `Vec String Number` has zero binders too. So
-- groundness is one condition of six, and the load-bearing one is the last:
--
--   1. the occurrence's route is a REGISTERED target that resolves in the
--      published scheme table;
--   2. the scheme carries zero quantified binders;
--   3. it carries no diagnostics — a fenced or advisory row is not evidence;
--   4. its body is an exact top-level arrow;
--   5. SC-eff — the target is pure, and a non-Fenel-bodied root is positively
--      ATTESTED; `requires` absence never proves purity, because a bodyless
--      declaration with no `@requires` can still perform network IO;
--   6. the exactness fence — the scheme projects through `typeToSetExpr` to a
--      `SeArrow` carrying no `SeAll` anywhere.
--
-- CONDITION 6 IS NOT INVENTED HERE. It is `synthFromSig`'s shipped rule: if the
-- projected signature carries `SeAll` anywhere, that is the marshal's
-- I-don't-know and the call exposes nothing. Reusing the checker's own
-- exactness predicate is deliberate — this entry must not be more permissive
-- than the checker that will consume what it admits.
--
-- AND CONDITION 6 IS WHAT FENCES THE VALUE-INDEXED SIGNATURES. `typeToSetExpr`
-- has no `TtApp` / `TtEl` arm and degrades to `SeAll`, so EVERY nominal
-- application — quantified or not — is refused here. That is the derived
-- reason the eleven `Vec`-argued declarations cannot reach this reader, and it
-- is pinned by a test rather than asserted. The earlier draft claimed they were
-- safe because they are quantified; `Vec String Number` is the zero-binder
-- counterexample that killed it, and this entry is a NEW scheme reader that
-- does not pass through `fnTypeMarker`'s refusal at all. The authored fence
-- itself is still owed before that refusal is replaced; this slice does not
-- replace it.
--
-- SC-SIG DOES NOT SURVIVE GROUNDNESS EITHER. Groundness removes instantiation,
-- not declaration trust — a lying ground declaration would validate every one
-- of its callers, which is the circularity P4 exists to break. So an admitted
-- occurrence additionally carries an exact `OccurrenceAuthority` drawn from the
-- supplied certificate set. No certificate is a fence. A current-SCC use fences
-- outright: discharging one needs the atomic group solve this slice does not
-- have, and an unvalidated assumption is not evidence.
--
-- BINDING IS TWO-LEVEL, BECAUSE ONE LEVEL CANNOT SEE MEANING. A digest over
-- (useId, snapshot, schemeHash, instance) binds MUTATION: an entry cannot be
-- re-targeted or have its arrow swapped. It does not bind MEANING — a run with
-- a matching dependency certificate and a run with none share that tuple
-- exactly and mean opposite things. So the per-entry digest covers the
-- authority and the resolved occurrence fields `VcaUseId` omits (saturation,
-- locality, enters-target — all three flip under an arity-table change), and an
-- outer admission digest covers the caller, the snapshot, the readiness, the
-- eligibility, the certificate set and the entry-digest vector as a table root.
--
-- HONEST LIMIT, AND IT IS IN THE TCB LEDGER RATHER THAN IMPLIED AWAY. These
-- digests are BINDING, not UNFORGEABLE. Any producer holding the authentic
-- material can recompute them: Fenel has no secret and an ADT is not a
-- capability. What they buy is that the parts cannot be supplied SEPARATELY,
-- which is the property actually needed here. A genuinely unforgeable seam
-- needs a private host capability, and that is reserved, not delivered.
--
-- Constructor names carry the Vte/Vta/Vtc/Vtl prefixes because constructor ids
-- are repo-global.
-- =========================================================================

-- ---- the request ---------------------------------------------------------

-- One published scheme row. `quantified` is the binder count rather than the
-- binder list because nothing here needs the binders: a nonzero count fences.
-- `diagnostics` is the authored diagnostic vector — nonempty means the row is
-- fenced or advisory, which is not evidence regardless of its shape.
VteSchemeRow = VteSchemeRow targetId: String schemeHash: ValidationHash quantified: Nat diagnostics: [String] outcome: String arrow: TraversalType requires: [String] origin: String attestedPure: Boolean attestedTotal: Boolean attestedHonest: Boolean sccId: String

-- One certificate the caller has already verified, projected to its join key.
-- `kind` is the closed authority discriminator: `dependency`, `primitive`,
-- `constructor` or `intrinsic`.
VteCertificateRow = VteCertificateRow targetId: String kind: String certificateHash: ValidationHash schemeHash: ValidationHash implementationHash: ValidationHash ruleVersion: String

-- The caller's own effective scheme. It is a REQUEST field rather than a
-- lookup because a caller validating itself must not resolve itself through
-- the same table it is being validated against.
VteCallerScheme = VteCallerScheme schemeHash: ValidationHash quantified: Nat diagnostics: [String] outcome: String arrow: TraversalType requires: [String] sccId: String sccSize: Nat

-- THE EXECUTOR PROFILE, A FIRST-CLASS COORDINATE (the term-substrate ADR's
-- §5.3 obligation, landing in the first successor slice that touches
-- `VteRequest`). The ADR's §1.5 divergence table pins seven forms — binding and
-- scoping forms, match binders, recursion and closure-head placement — on which
-- the two executors DISAGREE, and those are exactly the forms this slice
-- releases. An admission that does not name the profile it was taken under is
-- therefore ambiguous about the thing this slice makes checkable, so the
-- coordinate rides the request, the snapshot payload, both entry digests and
-- the outer admission digest.
--
-- IT IS CLOSED AND IT FAILS CLOSED. Only `VepPureEvaluate` is admitted: the
-- validation ledger mirrors the pure profile arm-for-arm
-- (`validation-ledger.fenel:13-39`), so a durable admission would be a lie
-- until §1.5 is reconciled or separately mirrored. There is no unknown arm and
-- no default — absence is a marshal error at the transport, which maps exactly
-- these two spellings and refuses anything else. A third profile is a TYPE
-- change, not a spelling.
--
-- DISAMBIGUATION: the harness's existing `profile` field is the REGISTRY
-- profile (SYSTEM versus seed), a different axis entirely. Every name, reason
-- and doc line here says "executor profile".
VteExecutorProfile = VepPureEvaluate | VepDurableTurn

VteRequest = VteRequest callerId: String body: (JsonLogic Any) paramNames: [String] routes: [ValidationRoute] flatRoutes: [ValidationRoute] arities: [ValidationArity] provenance: LedgerBodyProvenance wireProjections: [ValidationWireProjection] ctorFields: [ValidationCtorFieldRow] schemes: [VteSchemeRow] certificates: [VteCertificateRow] callerScheme: VteCallerScheme registryGeneration: String validatorVersion: String executorProfile: VteExecutorProfile

-- ---- the admission -------------------------------------------------------

-- One admitted occurrence. `authority` is the exact `OccurrenceAuthority` the
-- certificate set supplied, carried BESIDE the instance rather than implied by
-- it, and `digest` binds the whole row.
--
-- SLICE 3h ADDS THREE FIELDS, AND THIS WAS THE LAST FREE MOMENT TO ADD THEM:
-- nothing consumed an admission before this slice, and from here a shape change
-- is a consumer break. All three are bound AT MINT (the mint may read request
-- tables — that is its job; the CHECKER may not) and covered by the entry
-- digest.
--
--   * `declaredArity` — the target's formal count from `request.arities`. It is
--     the RUNTIME STAGE BOUNDARY and it is not recoverable from the instance:
--     `foldTypes` builds nested unary arrows, so a 2-formal declaration and a
--     1-formal declaration returning a callable project to the SAME nested
--     shape. Every application rule keys on it.
--   * `origin` — the target's implementation origin, from the same scheme row
--     the mint already consults for SC-eff. Rule (c)'s side condition is on it:
--     leftovers-applied-to-result holds for Fenel bodies and closures and NOT
--     for natives, which receive the entire argument vector.
--   * `executorProfile` — O1. It is on the ENTRY as well as the request so
--     `vteEntryDigestOf` can re-derive the digest from the entry ALONE; the
--     preflight's whole point is comparing a derivation against a stored value,
--     and a re-derivation that had to reach back into the request would be
--     comparing the request against itself.
VteEntry = VteEntry useId: VcaUseId snapshot: VcaSnapshot schemeHash: ValidationHash instance: SetExpr declaredArity: Nat origin: String executorProfile: VteExecutorProfile saturation: UseSaturation locality: UseLocality entersTarget: Boolean authority: OccurrenceAuthority digest: ValidationHash

-- THE MINT PARTITIONS, IT DOES NOT MINT-ONE-AND-REFUSE-THREE. 3g's mint
-- refused every role that does not publish a scheme, which made a body with one
-- `let`-bound variable read, one constructor or one intrinsic unvalidatable —
-- the rules this slice adds would have been unreachable. The four classes:
--
--   * ENTRY-BEARING — a `VurCall`/`VurReference` occurrence of kind `function`.
--     Minted `VteEntry` under the six conditions plus SC-sig, 3g's semantics
--     unchanged, now including OVER-saturated rows whose pending the classifier
--     must then discharge.
--   * Γ-CLASS — an ordinary lexical read. NO entry: the binder identity on the
--     row IS the evidence, consumed by the arm's Γ discipline.
--   * RULE-CLASS — a constructor or intrinsic occurrence. NO entry, because an
--     entry cannot be FORMED: a constructor has no scheme row, so `schemeHash`,
--     `instance` and `vteInstancesDerive` cannot be satisfied and inventing a
--     sentinel would fork the executors. It is discharged by D4's versioned
--     rule evidence, which rides facts already bound into the snapshot.
--   * FENCE — everything else, exactly as before.
--
-- The class is RE-DERIVED and never accepted. A caller who classified a
-- function identity as Γ-class to omit its entry is exactly the forged-carrier
-- attack this module's history documents, so the preflight recomputes every
-- class from the rebuilt carrier's own identities and refuses a disagreement.
VteClass = VtcEntryBearing | VtcGamma | VtcRule ruleKind: VteRuleKind

-- The closed rule vocabulary. A STRING here would have been the same
-- open-vocabulary hole the module already refuses elsewhere: a new spelling
-- would silently pick (or fail to pick) a checker rule.
VteRuleKind = VrkConstructor | VrkIntrinsic

VteClassified = VteClassified useId: VcaUseId class: VteClass

-- The closed admission. It is ONE value carrying everything the admission
-- rested on, so a consumer can re-derive `digest` from the admission's own
-- contents rather than trusting that it came from here. That is
-- self-authentication, not provenance: a recomputable digest cannot prove
-- origin. SLICE 3h wires the construct-and-consume seam: `classes` enumerates
-- every non-entry-bearing identity with its re-derived class (so the OUTER
-- digest covers them — an omitted Γ-class row is a different admission), and
-- the verdict rides the outcome rather than the admission because the
-- admission is what the verdict was computed FROM.
VteAdmission = VteAdmission callerId: String snapshot: VcaSnapshot eligibility: ValidationEligibility entries: [VteEntry] classes: [VteClassified] digest: ValidationHash

-- THE VERDICT IS REQUIRED, NOT OPTIONAL, and that is the fail-closed shape: an
-- admitted-without-verdict value is not constructible. A `Maybe` field or a
-- second arm would both leave "admitted, verdict unknown" expressible, and the
-- one thing this seam exists to prevent is an admission that nothing checked.
VteOutcome =
  VtaAdmitted admission: VteAdmission verdict: VchVerdict
  | VtaRefused reason: ValidationReason

-- ---- domain literals -----------------------------------------------------

-- EVERY CONSTANT BELOW TAKES A UNIT PARAMETER. A bare nullary declaration does
-- not evaluate to its value in argument or record-field position — it resolves
-- to a `{$ref}` handle, which reaches the certificate-byte leaf as a record
-- where a domain literal is required. The leaf REFUSED it, loudly, which is why
-- this was found at all; the same shape sitting silently in a digest payload
-- would have produced a stable digest of the wrong thing.
@label "Validation entry version"
@description "The trusted-entry construction version. It rides in every digest payload because an admission minted by a different construction is not evidence about this one. Bumped to v2 by slice 3h: the request grew two tables and a coordinate, the entry grew three fields, the mint partitions instead of refusing, readiness discharges one typed pending, and the admission carries classes and a verdict — a v1 digest and a v2 digest over the same-looking tuple mean different things."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteEntryVersion : (unit: a) -> String
vteEntryVersion unit = "fenel.validation.entry.v2"

@label "Validation inputs domain"
@description "The domain literal for the combined input digest — every table the carrier's answers depend on, bound together rather than individually."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteInputsDomain : (unit: a) -> String
vteInputsDomain unit = "fenel.validation.inputs.v1"

@label "Validation body domain"
@description "The domain literal for the recomputed body digest. The carrier copies its bodyHash from caller-supplied provenance and the provenance gate only checks non-emptiness, so the entry recomputes it and refuses a mismatch."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteBodyDomain : (unit: a) -> String
vteBodyDomain unit = "fenel.validation.body.v1"

@label "Validation occurrence domain"
@description "The domain literal for the per-occurrence entry digest — the occurrence identity, its resolved fields, its scheme identity, its instance AND its authority."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteOccurrenceDomain : (unit: a) -> String
vteOccurrenceDomain unit = "fenel.validation.occurrence.v1"

@label "Validation admission domain"
@description "The domain literal for the outer admission digest — caller, snapshot, eligibility and the entry-digest vector as a table root."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteAdmissionDomain : (unit: a) -> String
vteAdmissionDomain unit = "fenel.validation.admission.v1"

-- =========================================================================
-- the entry
-- =========================================================================

-- THE ORDER BELOW IS THE IMPLEMENTATION ORDER, and it is checked rather than
-- sketched: the design review's most embarrassing finding was three steps that
-- appeared in a step list and were never invoked.
@label "Trusted validation entry"
@description "The ONE authoritative validation entry. Takes a request and returns a closed outcome: it recomputes the body digest, refuses a caller whose own scheme is not ground-and-exact, builds the carrier itself from the frozen routes / flat routes / wire projections, refuses any carrier node that projects to an unsupported form, refuses a ledger that is not structurally ready, computes eligibility from inventory facts, and mints one table entry per live occurrence — each admitted only when its target's published scheme is ground, undiagnosed, an exact arrow, pure-or-attested, SeAll-free, and backed by an exact certificate. It takes NO table and has no fallback anywhere."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteValidate : (request: VteRequest) -> VteOutcome
vteValidate request =
  -- 1. THE BODY DIGEST IS DERIVED AND SUBSTITUTED, NOT MERELY COMPARED.
  --    `vlgProvenanceDivergent` checks only that the provenance fields are
  --    non-empty and that the parser is authoritative; nothing has ever hashed
  --    the body the caller actually supplied, so the snapshot's body identity
  --    was the caller's word.
  --
  --    Comparing would have been the weaker fix AND an unusable one: the digest
  --    is taken over the MARSHALLED body this entry walks, which a caller
  --    holding raw JSON-Logic cannot reproduce. So the derived digest REPLACES
  --    the claim in the provenance handed to the carrier, and nothing
  --    downstream rests on the caller for it. A caller that nevertheless states
  --    a body hash is still held to it — a disagreement means it believes it is
  --    validating a different body, which is worth refusing even though nothing
  --    downstream would have been wrong.
  let claimed = request.provenance
  let bodyDigest = validationCanonicalDigest (vteBodyDomain 0) request.body
  let provenance = LedgerBodyProvenance { bodyHash = bodyDigest.hex, astAbi = claimed.astAbi, parser = claimed.parser }
  if and (not (eq claimed.bodyHash "")) (not (eq claimed.bodyHash bodyDigest.hex))
  then VtaRefused { reason = RsnStaleEvidence { useId = request.callerId, observed = "the caller states a body hash that is not the digest of the body it supplied" } }
  -- 1b. THE EXECUTOR PROFILE (O1), refused before anything is built. The
  --     ledger and the carrier both mirror the PURE evaluator's reading of the
  --     binding, scoping, match-binder, recursion and closure-head forms; the
  --     durable turn disagrees on all seven (ADR §1.5). Admitting under a
  --     profile whose semantics nothing here models is not a coverage gap, it
  --     is a false statement about which program was checked.
  else
    case vteRequestRefusal request of
      Just reason -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
      Nothing ->
    -- 2. A caller whose own scheme is not ground-and-exact is refused BEFORE
    --    anything is built. A ground caller has no quantifiers, which is what
    --    makes the rigid and observation frames vacuous on every request this
    --    entry admits — and therefore what lets 3f's spelling-keyed
    --    `ObsCallTarget` obligation be discharged by fencing rather than left
    --    quietly unmet.
    case vteCallerRefusal request.callerId (length request.paramNames) request.callerScheme of
      Just reason -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
      Nothing ->
        let inputsHash = vteInputsHash request
        -- NAMED-FIELD patterns, deliberately, and the producer is why. Method
        -- note 5 of the brief records the inverse trap — `vlgLedger` applies
        -- `LgoLedger` POSITIONALLY, so a named pattern against it matches
        -- nothing and the body returns null with no error. `vcaCarrier` applies
        -- BOTH of its outcome constructors with NAMED fields
        -- (`VcoCarrier { carrier = … }`), so here the positional pattern is the
        -- one that silently matches nothing. It did, for one debugging round.
        -- Match a constructor the way its producer applies it.
        case vcaCarrier request.callerId request.body request.paramNames request.routes request.arities provenance request.flatRoutes request.wireProjections request.ctorFields inputsHash of
          VcoFenced { reason = reason } -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
          VcoCarrier { carrier = carrier } -> vteFromCarrier request carrier

@label "Trusted entry from carrier"
@description "The admission pipeline over a built carrier: the total projection walk, the whole-ledger readiness gate, the eligibility verdict, the per-occurrence mint, and the branded preflight. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteFromCarrier : (request: VteRequest) -> (carrier: VcaCarrier) -> VteOutcome
vteFromCarrier request carrier =
  -- 0. THE EXECUTOR PROFILE, AGAIN. This function and `vtePreflight` are
  --    separately callable and each rebuilds the carrier independently, so a
  --    gate placed only in `vteValidate` is bypassable: a durable request whose
  --    body is a literal has no entry whose profile field could catch the
  --    mismatch. Every admitting path carries the gate.
  case vteRequestRefusal request of
    Just reason -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
    Nothing ->
  -- 3. THE CARRIER IS REBUILT, NOT ACCEPTED — the same round-2 repair as
  --    `vtePreflight`. Binding a supplied carrier to the request (caller
  --    identity + the combined inputs digest) proves only that the supplier had
  --    the request, which it does by definition; the `identities`, the `root`
  --    and the `readiness` remain whatever it invented. Re-deriving readiness
  --    from a forged carrier's own rows proves the forgery is self-consistent
  --    and nothing else.
  --
  --    So the supplied carrier is checked for agreement and then DISCARDED in
  --    favour of one this function builds. On the honest path the two are the
  --    same value; on any other path the rebuilt one is authoritative.
  if not (eq carrier.callerId request.callerId)
  then VtaRefused { reason = RsnStaleEvidence { useId = request.callerId, observed = "the carrier names a different caller than the request" } }
  else if not (vcaHashEq carrier.snapshot.inputsHash (vteInputsHash request))
  then VtaRefused { reason = RsnStaleEvidence { useId = request.callerId, observed = "the carrier was not built from this request's inputs" } }
  else
    case vteCallerRefusal request.callerId (length request.paramNames) request.callerScheme of
      Just reason -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
      Nothing ->
        let claimed = request.provenance
        let bodyDigest = validationCanonicalDigest (vteBodyDomain 0) request.body
        let provenance = LedgerBodyProvenance { bodyHash = bodyDigest.hex, astAbi = claimed.astAbi, parser = claimed.parser }
        case vcaCarrier request.callerId request.body request.paramNames request.routes request.arities provenance request.flatRoutes request.wireProjections request.ctorFields (vteInputsHash request) of
          VcoFenced { reason = reason } -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
          VcoCarrier { carrier = rebuilt } -> vteFromCheckedCarrier request rebuilt

@label "Trusted entry from a request-bound carrier"
@description "The admission pipeline over a carrier already proven to belong to this request: the total projection walk, the whole-ledger readiness gate, the eligibility verdict, the per-occurrence mint, and the branded preflight. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteFromCheckedCarrier : (request: VteRequest) -> (carrier: VcaCarrier) -> VteOutcome
vteFromCheckedCarrier request carrier =
  -- 4. THE TOTAL PROJECTION WALK, and it closes a live hole rather than
  --    restating one. `vcaCollectFences` collects `VciFenced` and `VciAliased`
  --    only, and an unsupported STRUCTURAL node issues neither: an array
  --    literal, a `let`, a `do`, a `where`, an `ann`, a `withInstance` and a
  --    `typeDef` are all `VcpUnsupported` with identity `VciInert`, so 3f's
  --    preflight reaches `VckReady` with them present. Harmless while dormant;
  --    from this entry it is exactly the "hands evidence to a position the
  --    carrier has just said it cannot read" failure, reached through the door
  --    `vcaFirstUnsupportedIdentity` does not watch.
  case vteFirstUnsupported carrier.root of
    Just bad ->
      let projection = bad.projection
      VtaRefused { reason = RsnUnsupportedSyntax { form = vteProjectionForm projection, path = bad.path } }
    Nothing ->
      -- 5. Whole-ledger readiness. SLICE 3h STAGES THE DISCHARGE ORDER, and the
      --    staging is the narrowest one that makes the checker's rules
      --    reachable: `VrpExactOrigin` and `VrpNamedArgumentDecision` still
      --    refuse HERE, unchanged, because nothing in this slice supplies exact
      --    origin evidence or the runtime's reorder decision. Only
      --    `VrpCertifiedEtaStage` becomes DISCHARGEABLE — structural readiness,
      --    then mint, then the pre-checker classifier proves each eta-stage
      --    pending from the minted entry or refuses it closed. A pending
      --    nothing discharged does not become admissible by having been
      --    classified; the classifier is a proof obligation, not a bucket.
      case vteReadinessRefusal carrier of
        Just reason -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
        Nothing ->
          if gt (length carrier.fences) 0
          then VtaRefused { reason = RsnCheckerAbstained { detail = "the carrier recorded at least one fenced occurrence" } }
          else if vcaIdentitiesCollide carrier.identities
          then VtaRefused { reason = RsnUnsupportedSyntax { form = "carrier-duplicate-occurrence-identity", path = [] } }
          else
            -- 6. Eligibility, from INVENTORY facts only. Anti-circularity: a
            --    function is not eligible because something about it happened
            --    to verify.
            let eligibility = vteEligible request carrier.identities
            case eligibility of
              EligUnsupported { reason = reason } -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
              EligEvidenceIncomplete { reason = reason } -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
              EligEvidenceComplete -> vteMintTable request carrier

-- ---- the readiness gate --------------------------------------------------

@label "Trusted entry readiness refusal"
@description "The refusal reason for a whole-ledger readiness this slice cannot proceed from: a final fence, or any pending row whose typed demand nothing here can discharge. The ONE dischargeable demand is the certified eta-stage, which the pre-checker classifier must then actually prove; every other kind refuses exactly as a final fence does."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteReadinessRefusal : (carrier: VcaCarrier) -> Maybe ValidationReason
vteReadinessRefusal carrier =
  case carrier.readiness of
    VrdFinalFence { reason = reason } -> Just reason
    VrdNeedsRefinement { pending = pending } -> vteFirstUndischargeable pending
    VrdStructurallyReady -> Nothing
    _ -> Just (RsnCheckerAbstained { detail = "an unrecognised whole-ledger readiness judgment" })

@label "Trusted entry first undischargeable pending"
@description "The first typed pending demand this slice cannot carry past readiness, or Nothing when every pending row is a certified eta-stage. An unrecognised kind refuses — the safe default, written down rather than inferred from a missing arm."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteFirstUndischargeable : (pending: [VckPending]) -> Maybe ValidationReason
vteFirstUndischargeable pending =
  case head pending of
    Nothing -> Nothing
    Just p ->
      case p.kind of
        VrpCertifiedEtaStage -> vteFirstUndischargeable (eufRest pending)
        VrpExactOrigin -> Just (RsnMissingEvidence { useId = p.useId, observed = concat "an unmet evidence demand: " p.detail })
        VrpNamedArgumentDecision -> Just (RsnMissingEvidence { useId = p.useId, observed = concat "an unmet evidence demand: " p.detail })
        _ -> Just (RsnMissingEvidence { useId = p.useId, observed = concat "an unclassified evidence demand: " p.detail })

-- ---- the caller gate -----------------------------------------------------

@label "Trusted entry caller refusal"
@description "The refusal reason for a caller whose own effective scheme cannot ground a validation: an unsolved outcome, any authored diagnostic, a quantified scheme, a non-arrow body, an inexact projection, an effectful scheme, or membership in a multi-member SCC. Nothing when the caller is admissible."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteCallerRefusal : (callerId: String) -> (declaredArity: Nat) -> (scheme: VteCallerScheme) -> Maybe ValidationReason
vteCallerRefusal callerId declaredArity scheme =
  if not (eq scheme.outcome "Solved")
  then Just (RsnMissingEvidence { useId = callerId, observed = concat "the caller's own scheme is not Solved: " scheme.outcome })
  else if gt (length scheme.diagnostics) 0
  then Just (RsnMissingEvidence { useId = callerId, observed = "the caller's own scheme carries authored diagnostics" })
  else if not (eq scheme.quantified 0)
  -- COMPARED TO ZERO, not tested with `gt`. `not (gt n 0)` admits a NEGATIVE
  -- n, and nothing between the transport and here proves a `Nat`-declared slot
  -- actually holds one — the transport copies a host number verbatim. A gate
  -- that passes on out-of-range data is worse than no gate.
  -- A quantified caller needs the rigid frame and the observation fence, and
  -- the observation target table is still keyed by raw SPELLING — two
  -- same-spelled quantified occurrences would share the first target's result
  -- rigids. Fencing here is what makes that obligation discharged rather than
  -- skipped.
  then Just (RsnUnsupportedRankOrKind { detail = "a quantified caller scheme needs the occurrence-keyed observation frame, which is not converted" })
  else if gt (length scheme.requires) 0
  then Just (RsnEffectfulScEff { requires = scheme.requires })
  else if not (eq scheme.sccSize 1)
  then Just (RsnSccAssumptionNotValidated { sccId = scheme.sccId })
  else vteArrowRefusal callerId declaredArity scheme.arrow

-- THE CALLER'S OWN FORMALS NEED THE DOTTED FENCE TOO, and this is the door the
-- lambda and binding fences do not cover. `vlgLedger` seeds its binder scope
-- from `paramNames` VERBATIM, so a formal spelled `"x.y"` becomes a lexical
-- binder and a body's `{var:"x.y"}` joins it as an ordinary Γ-class read — while
-- the runtime's `resolveVar` splits the read into `x` then `y` and resolves
-- neither to the parameter. Seeding Γ with a name no read can reach would bind
-- the declared domain to a slot the runtime never fills.
@label "Trusted entry dotted formal"
@description "The first caller formal whose spelling carries the runtime's path separator, or Nothing when none does. A dotted formal is a binding no variable read can name, because resolveVar splits the spelling before consulting the binder scope."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteDottedFormal : (paramNames: [String]) -> Maybe String
vteDottedFormal paramNames =
  case head paramNames of
    Nothing -> Nothing
    Just n -> if vlgIsDotted n then Just n else vteDottedFormal (eufRest paramNames)

@label "Trusted entry arrow refusal"
@description "The refusal reason for a declared type that is not an EXACT arrow: one that does not project to a SeArrow, or one whose projection carries SeAll anywhere. The SeAll test is `synthFromSig`'s own exactness rule — a free variable, an $el, a nominal application or an open map all degrade to the top set, and the top set is the marshal saying it does not know."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteArrowRefusal : (useId: String) -> (declaredArity: Nat) -> (arrow: TraversalType) -> Maybe ValidationReason
vteArrowRefusal useId declaredArity arrow =
  let projected = vteInstanceArrow declaredArity arrow
  -- CONDITION ONE IS CHECKER PARITY. `seHasAll` is `synthFromSig`'s own rule,
  -- and keeping it means this entry can never be more permissive than the
  -- checker that will consume what it admits.
  if seHasAll projected
  then Just (RsnUnsupportedRankOrKind { detail = concat "the declared type projects to a set carrying SeAll, so it is not exact: " useId })
  -- CONDITION TWO IS STRICTLY STRONGER, AND IT HAS TO BE. "Contains no SeAll"
  -- is not "is exact": `SeWhere`, `SePattern` and `SeRigid` are all non-SeAll
  -- and none of them denotes a set this entry can stand behind. `SeRef` is the
  -- fourth member of that family and is ADMITTED anyway, under a stated side
  -- condition — see the walk below for why fencing it is not an option.
  else if vteResidual projected
  then Just (RsnUnsupportedRankOrKind { detail = concat "the declared type projects to a set carrying an unresolved or opaque residual, so it is not exact: " useId })
  else if not (eq (seTag projected) "SeArrow")
  then Just (RsnUnsupportedRankOrKind { detail = concat "the declared type does not project to an arrow: " useId })
  else Nothing

-- THE RESIDUAL WALK IS TOTAL OVER ALL FIFTEEN SetExpr CASES, and it ends in a
-- wildcard that answers TRUE.
--
-- The first version ended with no wildcard, and the comment above it claimed
-- that this was what stopped a sixteenth constructor from defaulting to
-- admissible. That was exactly backwards, and the round-2 review is what
-- surfaced it: a Fenel `case` with no matching arm falls through to host-null,
-- and null is not `true`, so an unrecognised constructor read as NOT a residual
-- — admissible. The safe default has to be written down, not inferred from the
-- absence of one. Every known case is still enumerated explicitly, so the
-- wildcard is unreachable today and is there for the constructor nobody has
-- added yet.
--
-- The direction is deliberate: a leaf this entry cannot READ is a residual,
-- whether or not a decider elsewhere can do something useful with it.
--
--   * `SeAll` — the top set; already caught by `seHasAll`, repeated here so
--     this walk stands alone.
--   * `SeWhere` — an opaque JSON-Logic predicate. Fencing it OVER-fences every
--     refined type, and that is the safe direction; admitting an arbitrary
--     predicate as exact would be trusting a body this entry never examines.
--   * `SePattern` — a regex/pattern algebra whose own nodes carry nested
--     SetExprs this walk would have to descend through a second vocabulary.
--   * `SeRigid` — the validation-only atom. It is minted only inside a
--     validation frame, and the arrow reaching this entry is CALLER-SUPPLIED,
--     so a rigid arriving here is either forged or leaked. Either way it is
--     not something a published signature may contain.
--
-- `SeEnum` / `SeInterval` / `SeNone` are exact finite or ordered leaves.
--
-- `SeRef` IS ADMITTED, AND THAT IS A SIDE CONDITION RATHER THAN A CLAIM. The
-- result review's counterexample is real: an arrow over an UNREGISTERED nominal
-- name projects to `SeRef "Ghost"`, which carries no `SeAll`, so nothing here
-- distinguishes it from a name that resolves. Fencing every `SeRef` was tried
-- and is not an option — `Int` itself projects to `SeRef "ℤ"`, so the rule
-- refuses every ordinary signature and the entry admits nothing at all.
--
-- Deciding it properly needs a "this handle resolves" predicate, and there is
-- none reachable from a body: named-set knowledge is split between the Fenel
-- Membership registry and the un-ported TS `NAMED_SETS`, and `namedMember`
-- answers per-VALUE rather than per-name. So the admission INHERITS closed-world
-- name resolution — the same assumption the deciders already make, where an
-- unresolvable handle answers `Nothing` at consumption rather than a verdict.
-- Recorded as a side condition on `vte.ground.exact`, not closed. The honest
-- statement: this entry is no more permissive than the checker on `SeRef`, and
-- strictly stricter on the four leaves above.
@label "Trusted entry residual"
@description "Does this projected set carry any residual the trusted entry cannot read — the top set, an unresolved name handle, an opaque predicate, a pattern algebra, or a validation rigid? Total over all fifteen SetExpr cases, written without a wildcard so a new constructor cannot default to admissible."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteResidual : (s: SetExpr) -> Boolean
vteResidual s =
  case s of
    SeAll -> true
    SeNone -> false
    SeEnum -> false
    SeInterval -> false
    SeRef -> false
    SePattern -> true
    SeWhere -> true
    SeRigid -> true
    SeCtor { ctor = ctor, fields = fields } -> vteResidualFields fields
    SeMu { var = var, body = body } -> vteResidual body
    SeArrow { params = params, returns = returns } ->
      if vteResidualParams params then true else vteResidual returns
    SeUnion { branches = branches } -> vteResidualList branches
    SeIntersect { branches = branches } -> vteResidualList branches
    SeDiff { left = left, right = right } ->
      if vteResidual left then true else vteResidual right
    SeComplement { inner = inner } -> vteResidual inner
    -- Unreachable today; the safe answer for a constructor added later.
    _ -> true

@label "Trusted entry residual in a branch list"
@description "Residual walk over a branch list. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteResidualList : (branches: List SetExpr) -> Boolean
vteResidualList branches =
  case head branches of
    Nothing -> false
    Just b -> if vteResidual b then true else vteResidualList (eufRest branches)

@label "Trusted entry residual in constructor fields"
@description "Residual walk over a constructor's field sets. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteResidualFields : (fields: List SetCtorField) -> Boolean
vteResidualFields fields =
  case head fields of
    Nothing -> false
    Just f -> if vteResidual f.set then true else vteResidualFields (eufRest fields)

@label "Trusted entry residual in arrow params"
@description "Residual walk over an arrow's parameter sets. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteResidualParams : (params: List SetArrowParam) -> Boolean
vteResidualParams params =
  case head params of
    Nothing -> false
    Just p -> if vteResidual p.set then true else vteResidualParams (eufRest params)

-- ---- the executor profile (O1) -------------------------------------------

-- THE REQUEST-LEVEL GATE, ON EVERY ADMITTING PATH. `vteValidate`,
-- `vteFromCarrier` and `vtePreflight` are each separately callable and each
-- rebuilds the carrier independently, so a gate in one of them is not a gate.
@label "Trusted entry request refusal"
@description "The refusal reason for a request this entry cannot validate at all, independently of its body: an executor profile whose semantics nothing here models, or a declared formal whose spelling the runtime's variable resolution splits. Nothing when the request itself is admissible."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteRequestRefusal : (request: VteRequest) -> Maybe ValidationReason
vteRequestRefusal request =
  if vteProfileRefused request.executorProfile
  then Just (RsnExecutorProfileUnsupported { profile = vteExecutorProfileKey request.executorProfile })
  else
    case vteDottedFormal request.paramNames of
      Just name -> Just (RsnUnsupportedSyntax { form = concat "caller-formal-dotted:" name, path = [] })
      Nothing -> Nothing

@label "Trusted entry executor profile refused"
@description "Is this executor profile one the entry cannot admit? Only the pure evaluate profile is admitted: the ledger and the carrier mirror its reading of the binding, scoping, match-binder, recursion and closure-head forms, and the durable turn diverges on all of them. No unknown arm and no default — the field is a closed ADT and the transport refuses anything else."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteProfileRefused : (profile: VteExecutorProfile) -> Boolean
vteProfileRefused profile =
  case profile of
    VepPureEvaluate -> false
    VepDurableTurn -> true
    -- Unreachable over the closed ADT; written down because a `case` with no
    -- matching arm falls through to host-null, which reads FALSE — i.e.
    -- ADMITTED. The safe default has to be stated, not inferred.
    _ -> true

@label "Trusted entry executor profile key"
@description "The closed tag of an executor profile, for digest payloads and refusal reasons. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteExecutorProfileKey : (profile: VteExecutorProfile) -> String
vteExecutorProfileKey profile =
  case profile of
    VepPureEvaluate -> "VepPureEvaluate"
    VepDurableTurn -> "VepDurableTurn"
    _ -> "VepUnknown"

-- ---- the arity-aware instance projection ---------------------------------

-- ONE PROJECTION HELPER, THREE CALL SITES, SO THE TWO PROJECTIONS CANNOT
-- DISAGREE. `foldTypes [ret] = ret`, so a ZERO-FORMAL target's published scheme
-- projects to whatever its RETURN type projects to — a non-arrow for an
-- ordinary return, and (worse) a perfectly well-formed `SeArrow` when the
-- return type is itself a function. The first shape refused at
-- `vteArrowRefusal`; the second would have been admitted as a ONE-formal
-- target, which is a different function.
--
-- So for `declaredArity = 0` the projection is wrapped in the EXPLICIT
-- zero-parameter arrow UNCONDITIONALLY — not "when it is not already an arrow",
-- which is the version that reads a 0-formal `() -> (B -> C)` as a 1-formal
-- `(b: B) -> C`. The same normalization runs at the mint's instance build, at
-- `vteInstancesDerive`'s re-projection, and at the caller gate, because a
-- preflight that re-projected differently from the mint would reject the
-- wrapper the mint built.
--
-- This is the ONE sanctioned deviation from the build-through-`foldTypes` rule,
-- and it is recorded as such in the `vte.entry.fields` ledger row.
@label "Trusted entry instance arrow"
@description "The projected instance for a declared type at a known formal count: typeToSetProjection widened to SeAll on degradation, with a ZERO-formal declaration wrapped in an explicit zero-parameter arrow over its projected return. foldTypes erases the difference between a 0-formal target and its return type, and the arity table is the only thing that can restore it."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteInstanceArrow : (declaredArity: Nat) -> (arrow: TraversalType) -> SetExpr
vteInstanceArrow declaredArity arrow =
  let projection = typeToSetProjection arrow
  let projected = if projection.degraded then SeAll else projection.set
  if eq declaredArity 0
  then SeArrow { params = [], returns = projected }
  else projected

-- The number of PARAMETER SLOTS a projected arrow chain exposes, counted down
-- the nested-unary spine rather than off one layer. `arrowDropParams` drops
-- from ONE layer and computes the wrong residual on a chain, which is why this
-- counts its own way.
@label "Trusted entry arrow chain length"
@description "How many parameter slots a projected arrow chain exposes in total, summed layer by layer down the nested unary spine that foldTypes builds. A non-arrow is zero."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteArrowChainLength : (s: SetExpr) -> Nat
vteArrowChainLength s =
  case s of
    SeArrow { params = params, returns = returns } -> add (length params) (vteArrowChainLength returns)
    _ -> 0

-- AMBIGUITY IS NOTHING, NOT THE FIRST MATCH — `vteSchemeFor`'s discipline, and
-- it is needed here for the same reason: `vlgLookupArity` is FIRST-MATCH, so a
-- table naming one target twice would make the declared arity depend on table
-- order, and the declared arity is the runtime stage boundary every application
-- rule keys on.
@label "Trusted entry arity for target"
@description "The declared formal count for a target id, or Nothing when the arity table names it zero times or more than once. Ambiguity is Nothing rather than the first match: the declared arity is the runtime stage boundary, so an order-dependent answer is not an answer."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteArityFor : (arities: [ValidationArity]) -> (targetId: String) -> Maybe Nat
vteArityFor arities targetId =
  if not (eq (vteArityCount arities targetId 0) 1)
  then Nothing
  else vteArityFirst arities targetId

@label "Trusted entry arity count"
@description "How many arity rows name one target. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteArityCount : (arities: [ValidationArity]) -> (targetId: String) -> (acc: Nat) -> Nat
vteArityCount arities targetId acc =
  case head arities of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just entry ->
      let next = if eq entry.targetId targetId then add acc 1 else acc
      vteArityCount (eufRest arities) targetId next

@label "Trusted entry arity first"
@description "The first arity row naming a target, called only after the count is exactly one. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteArityFirst : (arities: [ValidationArity]) -> (targetId: String) -> Maybe Nat
vteArityFirst arities targetId =
  case head arities of
    Nothing -> Nothing
    Just entry ->
      if eq entry.targetId targetId
      then Just entry.arity
      else vteArityFirst (eufRest arities) targetId

-- THE INSTANCE KEY IS A STRUCTURAL PROJECTION, not a tag. An entry digest that
-- carried only `seTag instance` could not tell `String -> String` from
-- `Number -> Number`: both are `SeArrow`, so two materially different instances
-- collided under one digest. The walk below is total over the same fifteen
-- cases and emits a labelled, canonically-encodable record — no rendering of
-- numbers into strings, because a cloned decider context boxes host numbers.
@label "Trusted entry set key"
@description "A total, canonically-encodable structural key for one projected set — enough to distinguish two instances that share a constructor tag. Used as digest material, never as an identity by itself."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteSetKey : (s: SetExpr) -> a
vteSetKey s =
  case s of
    SeAll -> { tag = "SeAll", name = "", kids = [] }
    SeNone -> { tag = "SeNone", name = "", kids = [] }
    SeEnum { values = values } -> { tag = "SeEnum", name = "", kids = values }
    SeInterval -> { tag = "SeInterval", name = "", kids = [] }
    SeRef { handle = handle } -> { tag = "SeRef", name = handle, kids = [] }
    SePattern -> { tag = "SePattern", name = "", kids = [] }
    SeWhere -> { tag = "SeWhere", name = "", kids = [] }
    SeRigid { scopeToken = scopeToken, ordinal = ordinal, kind = kind } ->
      { tag = "SeRigid", name = "", kids = [ordinal] }
    SeCtor { ctor = ctor, fields = fields } ->
      { tag = "SeCtor", name = ctor, kids = vteSetKeyFields fields [] }
    SeMu { var = var, body = body } ->
      { tag = "SeMu", name = var, kids = [vteSetKey body] }
    SeArrow { params = params, returns = returns } ->
      { tag = "SeArrow", name = "", kids = appendItem (vteSetKey returns) (vteSetKeyParams params []) }
    SeUnion { branches = branches } -> { tag = "SeUnion", name = "", kids = vteSetKeyList branches [] }
    SeIntersect { branches = branches } -> { tag = "SeIntersect", name = "", kids = vteSetKeyList branches [] }
    SeDiff { left = left, right = right } ->
      { tag = "SeDiff", name = "", kids = [vteSetKey left, vteSetKey right] }
    SeComplement { inner = inner } -> { tag = "SeComplement", name = "", kids = [vteSetKey inner] }
    -- Same reasoning as `vteResidual`'s tail: without this arm an unrecognised
    -- constructor would key as host-null and two different unknowns would share
    -- a digest. Unreachable today.
    _ -> { tag = "SeUnknown", name = "", kids = [] }

@label "Trusted entry set keys in a branch list"
@description "Structural keys for a branch list, in order. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteSetKeyList : (branches: List SetExpr) -> (acc: a) -> a
vteSetKeyList branches acc =
  case head branches of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just b -> vteSetKeyList (eufRest branches) (appendItem (vteSetKey b) acc)

@label "Trusted entry set keys in constructor fields"
@description "Structural keys for a constructor's fields, carrying the wire name so two same-shaped fields under different names do not collide. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteSetKeyFields : (fields: List SetCtorField) -> (acc: a) -> a
vteSetKeyFields fields acc =
  case head fields of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just f -> vteSetKeyFields (eufRest fields) (appendItem { wire = f.wireName, set = vteSetKey f.set } acc)

@label "Trusted entry set keys in arrow params"
@description "Structural keys for an arrow's parameters, carrying the wire name and position. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteSetKeyParams : (params: List SetArrowParam) -> (acc: a) -> a
vteSetKeyParams params acc =
  case head params of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just p -> vteSetKeyParams (eufRest params) (appendItem { wire = p.wireName, set = vteSetKey p.set } acc)

-- ---- the total projection walk -------------------------------------------

@label "Trusted entry first unsupported node"
@description "The first carrier node whose projection is not a supported core form, in walk order. The invariant is `every node's projection is supported; a node that BEARS an identity additionally carries an admissible occurrence` — an unsupported node is refused whether or not it issued an identity, because an unsupported node that issued none is invisible to the carrier's own fence collection."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteFirstUnsupported : (node: VcaNode) -> Maybe VcaNode
vteFirstUnsupported node =
  if not (vteProjectionSupported node.projection)
  then Just node
  else vteFirstUnsupportedKids node.children

@label "Trusted entry first unsupported in children"
@description "Fold the unsupported-projection search over a bundled child list. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteFirstUnsupportedKids : (children: [VcaChild]) -> Maybe VcaNode
vteFirstUnsupportedKids children =
  case head children of
    Nothing -> Nothing
    Just c ->
      case vteFirstUnsupported c.node of
        Just bad -> Just bad
        Nothing -> vteFirstUnsupportedKids (eufRest children)

@label "Trusted entry projection supported"
@description "Is this carrier projection one of the core forms a consumer may read? The `VcpUnsupported` set is deliberately conservative — `$` applications, `let`, `do` and arrays are fenced for COVERAGE rather than because the checker cannot express them — and widening it is a coverage decision that belongs to a checker-rule slice, not to a reader."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteProjectionSupported : (projection: VcaProjection) -> Boolean
vteProjectionSupported projection =
  case projection of
    VcpLit -> true
    VcpVar -> true
    VcpCall -> true
    VcpConstruct -> true
    VcpRecord -> true
    VcpLambda -> true
    VcpMatch -> true
    VcpPattern -> true
    -- SLICE 3h widens exactly ONE family. `let` / `where` / `do` / a `do`
    -- item's inline `let` now carry the closed `VcpBinding` projection, and the
    -- checker threads Γ through them in the pure profile's order. Nothing else
    -- moves: `array-literal`, `dollar-application`, `indirect-callee`,
    -- `expression-callee`, `absent-callee`, `ann`, `withInstance`, `typeDef`,
    -- `method`, `dynamic-apply:*`, `env-path:*`, `raw-rule:*` and
    -- `match-without-scrutinee` stay fenced with their own reasons.
    VcpBinding -> true
    VcpUnsupported -> false

@label "Trusted entry projection form"
@description "The closed form label of an unsupported projection, for the refusal reason. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteProjectionForm : (projection: VcaProjection) -> String
vteProjectionForm projection =
  case projection of
    VcpUnsupported { form = form } -> concat "carrier-unsupported-projection:" form
    _ -> "carrier-unsupported-projection"

-- ---- eligibility (D5) ----------------------------------------------------

-- ELIGIBILITY IS COMPUTED FROM INVENTORY FACTS ALONE. That is the
-- anti-circularity property slice 2b established and it is easy to lose: a
-- classifier that consulted a certificate would make "eligible" mean "happened
-- to verify", and the partition would stop being able to say that an
-- unattested root is fenced.
@label "Trusted entry eligibility"
@description "The eligibility verdict for one caller, from inventory facts only — never from a certificate, a verdict or a trust tag. A caller whose own scheme is unsolved, diagnosed, quantified, effectful or inexact is EligUnsupported; a caller whose body carries a use with no published scheme row is EligEvidenceIncomplete; otherwise EligEvidenceComplete."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteEligible : (request: VteRequest) -> (identities: [VcaUseId]) -> ValidationEligibility
vteEligible request identities =
  case vteCallerRefusal request.callerId (length request.paramNames) request.callerScheme of
    Just reason -> EligUnsupported { reason = reason }
    Nothing ->
      -- The SECOND verdict, and without it this classifier would be a
      -- restatement of the caller gate the entry has already run — i.e. always
      -- `EligEvidenceComplete` by the time it is consulted, which is a verdict
      -- that says nothing. `EligEvidenceIncomplete` is the honest middle state:
      -- the caller itself is fine, and the body reaches a target the published
      -- inventory does not describe. It is computed from INVENTORY facts only —
      -- presence of a scheme row, never a certificate, a verdict or a trust tag
      -- — which is the anti-circularity property slice 2b established: a
      -- classifier that consulted a certificate would make "eligible" mean
      -- "happened to verify".
      case vteFirstUndescribed request identities of
        Just targetId -> EligEvidenceIncomplete { reason = RsnMissingEvidence { useId = targetId, observed = "the published scheme inventory does not describe a target this body reaches" } }
        Nothing -> EligEvidenceComplete

-- SLICE 3h MAKES THE SKIP EXPLICIT AND CHECKED. The 3g version skipped every
-- identity whose ROLE did not carry a scheme, which silently exempted
-- constructors, intrinsics and lexical reads from the inventory question
-- altogether while still demanding a scheme row for a `{call:["Ctor",…]}`
-- occurrence (role `VurCall`, resolved kind `constructor`) that has none. Both
-- halves were wrong in opposite directions.
--
-- Post-partition each class is asked the question it can answer, and every
-- answer is still an INVENTORY fact — never a certificate, a verdict or a trust
-- tag, so the anti-circularity property is preserved:
--
--   * ENTRY-BEARING — the published scheme table names the target exactly once
--     AND the arity table does. The second half is new and load-bearing: the
--     declared arity is the runtime stage boundary, so a target the arity table
--     cannot describe is a target the inventory does not describe.
--   * Γ-CLASS — its binder. The identity carries the binder id as its target,
--     minted by the ledger from the caller's own binder scope, so the
--     description is the row itself.
--   * RULE-CLASS — the frozen route row that resolved its kind, which rides
--     `inputsHash`. A construct-FORM occurrence additionally had an
--     authoritative source-to-wire row proved by the carrier before it could
--     issue an identity at all (`vcaWireVerdict` fences without one), so the
--     wire table is checked — upstream, unconditionally, and fail-closed.
--     Re-checking it here is not possible anyway: the wire table is keyed by
--     the SOURCE spelling while a constructor identity carries the OWNER-EXACT
--     semantic target.
--   * UNCLASSIFIABLE — undescribed, and the mint would refuse it regardless.
@label "Trusted entry first undescribed target"
@description "The first live occurrence the published inventory does not describe, or Nothing when it describes every one. Class-aware: an entry-bearing use needs an unambiguous scheme row AND arity row; a Γ-class read is described by its binder; a rule-class occurrence by the frozen route row that resolved its kind (with the constructor wire row already proved by the carrier). Inventory facts only. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteFirstUndescribed : (request: VteRequest) -> (identities: [VcaUseId]) -> Maybe String
vteFirstUndescribed request identities =
  case head identities of
    Nothing -> Nothing
    Just useId ->
      case vteClassOf useId of
        Nothing -> Just useId.targetId
        Just class ->
          case class of
            VtcEntryBearing ->
              case vteSchemeFor request.schemes useId.targetId of
                Nothing -> Just useId.targetId
                Just row ->
                  case vteArityFor request.arities useId.targetId of
                    Nothing -> Just useId.targetId
                    Just declaredArity -> vteFirstUndescribed request (eufRest identities)
            VtcGamma -> vteFirstUndescribed request (eufRest identities)
            VtcRule -> vteFirstUndescribed request (eufRest identities)
            _ -> Just useId.targetId

-- ---- the mint ------------------------------------------------------------

@label "Trusted entry mint table"
@description "Mint one table entry per live carrier identity, then run the branded preflight. The table is BUILT here and never accepted: this is the whole difference between the authoritative path and `vckPreflightTable`. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteMintTable : (request: VteRequest) -> (carrier: VcaCarrier) -> VteOutcome
vteMintTable request carrier =
  case vteMintGo request carrier carrier.identities [] of
    Err reason -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
    Ok entries ->
      case vteClassesGo carrier.identities [] of
        Err reason -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
        Ok classes -> vtePreflight request entries classes

-- ---- the partition -------------------------------------------------------

-- THE CLASS IS DERIVED FROM THE OCCURRENCE IDENTITY ALONE, and from BOTH
-- halves of it. The role is SYNTACTIC and the kind is RESOLVED, and only the
-- pair is meaningful: `vlgWalk` assigns `VurCall` to any named call while the
-- route table independently carries what that name resolved to, so a call whose
-- route kind is `constructor` reaches here with a role that looks
-- scheme-bearing. Reading the role alone admitted a constructor application as
-- an external dependency once already.
--
-- A pair nobody has classified is `Nothing`, i.e. REFUSED. The kind vocabulary
-- is the registry's, not this module's, so the safe default is to classify
-- nothing new.
@label "Trusted entry class of occurrence"
@description "The admission class of one occurrence identity, derived from the role/kind PAIR: an entry-bearing function use, a Γ-class lexical read, a rule-class constructor or intrinsic, or Nothing when the pair names no class this slice can carry. Nothing refuses; it never defaults."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteClassOf : (useId: VcaUseId) -> Maybe VteClass
vteClassOf useId =
  if eq useId.kind "function"
  then (if vteRoleCarriesScheme useId.role then Just VtcEntryBearing else Nothing)
  else if eq useId.kind "constructor"
  -- A `{construct: […]}` occurrence carries `VurConstructor`; a
  -- `{call: ["Ctor", …]}` carries `VurCall` with the SAME resolved kind. Both
  -- are constructor occurrences and both are rule-class.
  then (if vteRoleIsConstructorish useId.role then Just (VtcRule { ruleKind = VrkConstructor }) else Nothing)
  else if eq useId.kind "intrinsic"
  then (if vteRoleIsIntrinsicish useId.role then Just (VtcRule { ruleKind = VrkIntrinsic }) else Nothing)
  else if eq useId.kind "lexical"
  then (if eq useId.role "VurLexical" then Just VtcGamma else Nothing)
  else Nothing

@label "Trusted entry role is constructorish"
@description "Which syntactic roles can carry a resolved CONSTRUCTOR kind: the construct form's own role, and a named call whose spelling resolved to a constructor. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteRoleIsConstructorish : (role: String) -> Boolean
vteRoleIsConstructorish role =
  if eq role "VurConstructor" then true
  else if eq role "VurCall" then true
  else eq role "VurReference"

@label "Trusted entry role is intrinsicish"
@description "Which syntactic roles can carry a resolved INTRINSIC kind: the intrinsic form's own role, and a named call or reference whose spelling resolved to a language rule. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteRoleIsIntrinsicish : (role: String) -> Boolean
vteRoleIsIntrinsicish role =
  if eq role "VurIntrinsic" then true
  else if eq role "VurCall" then true
  else eq role "VurReference"

@label "Trusted entry classes worker"
@description "Enumerate every NON-entry-bearing identity with its re-derived class, refusing at the first identity whose role/kind pair names no class. Entry-bearing identities are omitted here because they are enumerated by their entries. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteClassesGo : (identities: [VcaUseId]) -> (acc: [VteClassified]) -> Result ValidationReason [VteClassified]
vteClassesGo identities acc =
  case head identities of
    Nothing -> Ok acc
    Just useId ->
      case vteClassOf useId of
        Nothing -> Err (RsnUnclassifiedOccurrence { useId = useId.targetId, detail = concat (concat "role=" useId.role) (concat " kind=" useId.kind) })
        Just class ->
          case class of
            VtcEntryBearing -> vteClassesGo (eufRest identities) acc
            _ -> vteClassesGo (eufRest identities) (appendItem (VteClassified { useId = useId, class = class }) acc)

@label "Trusted entry class key"
@description "The structured key of an admission class — the closed tag plus the rule kind for a rule-class row. Digest material. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteClassKey : (class: VteClass) -> a
vteClassKey class =
  case class of
    VtcEntryBearing -> { tag = "VtcEntryBearing", ruleKind = "" }
    VtcGamma -> { tag = "VtcGamma", ruleKind = "" }
    VtcRule { ruleKind = ruleKind } -> { tag = "VtcRule", ruleKind = vteRuleKindKey ruleKind }
    _ -> { tag = "VtcUnknown", ruleKind = "" }

@label "Trusted entry rule kind key"
@description "The closed tag of a rule kind. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteRuleKindKey : (kind: VteRuleKind) -> String
vteRuleKindKey kind =
  case kind of
    VrkConstructor -> "VrkConstructor"
    VrkIntrinsic -> "VrkIntrinsic"
    _ -> "VrkUnknown"

@label "Trusted entry class keys"
@description "The classified-identity vector as an ordered list of structured keys — the identity triple plus its re-derived class, so the OUTER admission digest covers every Γ-class and rule-class row. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteClassKeys : (classes: [VteClassified]) -> (acc: a) -> a
vteClassKeys classes acc =
  case head classes of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just row ->
      let useId = row.useId
      let key = {
            callerId = useId.callerId,
            role = useId.role,
            path = vteSegKeys useId.path [],
            targetId = useId.targetId,
            kind = useId.kind,
            class = vteClassKey row.class
          }
      vteClassKeys (eufRest classes) (appendItem key acc)

@label "Trusted entry mint worker"
@description "Mint entries for a live identity list, refusing at the FIRST occurrence that cannot be admitted. Refusing early rather than accumulating is deliberate: a partial table is not a weaker admission, it is a different one, and there is no such outcome. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteMintGo : (request: VteRequest) -> (carrier: VcaCarrier) -> (identities: [VcaUseId]) -> (acc: [VteEntry]) -> Result ValidationReason [VteEntry]
vteMintGo request carrier identities acc =
  case head identities of
    Nothing -> Ok acc
    Just useId ->
      -- SLICE 3h: the mint PARTITIONS. A Γ-class or rule-class identity is not
      -- an entry and not a refusal — it is skipped here and enumerated by
      -- `vteClassesGo`. Only an unclassifiable identity refuses.
      case vteClassOf useId of
        Nothing -> Err (RsnUnclassifiedOccurrence { useId = useId.targetId, detail = concat (concat "role=" useId.role) (concat " kind=" useId.kind) })
        Just class ->
          case class of
            VtcEntryBearing ->
              case vteMintOne request carrier useId of
                Err reason -> Err reason
                Ok entry -> vteMintGo request carrier (eufRest identities) (appendItem entry acc)
            _ -> vteMintGo request carrier (eufRest identities) acc

@label "Trusted entry mint one"
@description "Mint one occurrence's entry, or the exact reason it cannot be minted. Applies the six admission conditions in order — registered route, published scheme, zero binders, no diagnostics, exact SeAll-free arrow, SC-eff purity-or-attestation — then resolves the exact OccurrenceAuthority, then derives the instance from that same scheme row and digests the whole result."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteMintOne : (request: VteRequest) -> (carrier: VcaCarrier) -> (useId: VcaUseId) -> Result ValidationReason VteEntry
vteMintOne request carrier useId =
  -- Condition 1, gated on the ROLE rather than on a kind spelling. The kind
  -- string is copied from the frozen route table and its vocabulary is the
  -- registry's, not this module's — pinning an exact spelling here would make
  -- the entry silently stop admitting anything the day that vocabulary moved,
  -- or (worse) admit a kind nobody considered. The ROLE is a closed constructor
  -- the ledger derives, so it is the authoritative discriminator.
  --
  -- Only a call or a bare registered reference names a target with a published
  -- SCHEME. A lexical binder's monotype comes from a solve this slice does not
  -- run; a constructor and an intrinsic are RULE-shaped rather than
  -- scheme-shaped and belong to the checker-rule slice; a capability dispatch
  -- is scope-dependent by construction and can never be settled from a table.
  if not (vteRoleCarriesScheme useId.role)
  then Err (RsnUnsupportedRegisteredReference { useId = concat useId.targetId (concat " role=" useId.role) })
  else
    case vteSchemeFor request.schemes useId.targetId of
      Nothing -> Err (RsnMissingEvidence { useId = useId.targetId, observed = "no published scheme row for the occurrence's target" })
      Just row ->
        -- THE DECLARED ARITY IS RESOLVED BEFORE THE ARROW IS PROJECTED, because
        -- the projection DEPENDS on it (`vteInstanceArrow`'s zero-formal case).
        -- An ambiguous or absent arity row refuses: the declared arity is the
        -- runtime stage boundary, and every application rule keys on it.
        case vteArityFor request.arities useId.targetId of
          Nothing -> Err (RsnMissingEvidence { useId = useId.targetId, observed = "the arity table does not name the occurrence's target exactly once" })
          Just declaredArity ->
            case vteSchemeRefusal useId.targetId request.callerId request.callerScheme.sccId declaredArity row of
              Just reason -> Err reason
              Nothing ->
                let instance = vteInstanceArrow declaredArity row.arrow
                -- A DECLARED ARITY LONGER THAN THE CHAIN IS INCOHERENT, and
                -- silently truncating it would make the checker descend past
                -- the spine's end and synthesise a set for a stage that does
                -- not exist. The two tables disagree; that is a refusal.
                if gt declaredArity (vteArrowChainLength instance)
                then Err (RsnUnsupportedRankOrKind { detail = concat "the declared arity exceeds the target's projected arrow chain: " useId.targetId })
                else
                -- SC-SIG. Groundness removes instantiation, not declaration trust.
                case vteAuthorityFor request.certificates row of
                  Err reason -> Err reason
                  Ok authority ->
                    let siteRow = vteRowFor carrier.root useId
                    case siteRow of
                      Nothing -> Err (RsnMissingEvidence { useId = useId.targetId, observed = "the occurrence identity names no carrier row" })
                      Just row2 ->
                    -- THE ROLE IS SYNTACTIC AND THE KIND IS RESOLVED, and only
                    -- the pair is meaningful. `vlgWalk` assigns `VurCall` to any
                    -- named call while the route table independently carries
                    -- what that name RESOLVED to, so a call whose route kind is
                    -- `constructor` or `capability` reaches here with a role
                    -- that passes. Checking the role alone admitted a
                    -- constructor application as an external dependency.
                        if not (vteKindCarriesScheme useId.kind)
                        then Err (RsnUnsupportedRegisteredReference { useId = concat useId.targetId (concat " kind=" useId.kind) })
                        else Ok (vteBuildEntry request carrier useId row row2 authority declaredArity instance)

@label "Trusted entry role carries scheme"
@description "Does this occurrence role name a target that publishes a SCHEME? A call and a bare registered reference do; a constructor and an intrinsic are rule-shaped, a capability dispatch is scope-dependent, and a lexical binding's monotype comes from a solve. The role tag is the ledger's own closed discriminator, so it does not drift with the registry's kind vocabulary."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteRoleCarriesScheme : (role: String) -> Boolean
vteRoleCarriesScheme role =
  if eq role "VurCall" then true
  else eq role "VurReference"

@label "Trusted entry kind carries scheme"
@description "Does the RESOLVED route kind name a target that publishes a scheme? A constructor, a capability dispatch and an intrinsic rule do not — they are rule-shaped — and the syntactic role cannot distinguish them, because a named call to any of them still carries VurCall. An unrecognised kind is refused: the vocabulary is the registry's, so the safe default is to admit nothing new."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteKindCarriesScheme : (kind: String) -> Boolean
vteKindCarriesScheme kind =
  if eq kind "constructor" then false
  else if eq kind "capability" then false
  else if eq kind "intrinsic" then false
  else if eq kind "lexical" then false
  else if eq kind "" then false
  else eq kind "function"

@label "Trusted entry build entry"
@description "Assemble one admitted entry and its binding digest. The instance is the target's own published arrow projected through the SAME exactness path the refusal already checked — derived from the scheme row, never proposed. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteBuildEntry : (request: VteRequest) -> (carrier: VcaCarrier) -> (useId: VcaUseId) -> (scheme: VteSchemeRow) -> (row: UseLedgerRow) -> (authority: OccurrenceAuthority) -> (declaredArity: Nat) -> (instance: SetExpr) -> VteEntry
vteBuildEntry request carrier useId scheme row authority declaredArity instance =
  let locality = vteLocalityOf row
  let digest = vteEntryDigest carrier.snapshot useId scheme row locality authority instance declaredArity request.executorProfile
  VteEntry {
    useId = useId,
    snapshot = carrier.snapshot,
    schemeHash = scheme.schemeHash,
    instance = instance,
    declaredArity = declaredArity,
    origin = scheme.origin,
    executorProfile = request.executorProfile,
    saturation = row.saturation,
    locality = locality,
    entersTarget = row.entersTarget,
    authority = authority,
    digest = digest
  }

@label "Trusted entry locality of"
@description "The occurrence locality a ledger row describes. A resolved row naming a registered target is external here, because a current-SCC use has already been refused. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteLocalityOf : (row: UseLedgerRow) -> UseLocality
vteLocalityOf row =
  case row.status of
    UlsLexical -> VulLexical
    _ -> VulExternal

-- ---- the scheme conditions -----------------------------------------------

@label "Trusted entry scheme refusal"
@description "The refusal reason for a published scheme row that cannot supply a ground instance: it sits in the CALLER's own SCC, or is not Solved, or carries diagnostics, or is quantified, or is effectful (or a non-Fenel root without a positive attestation), or is not an exact SeAll-free arrow. Nothing when the row is admissible."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteSchemeRefusal : (targetId: String) -> (callerId: String) -> (callerSccId: String) -> (declaredArity: Nat) -> (row: VteSchemeRow) -> Maybe ValidationReason
vteSchemeRefusal targetId callerId callerSccId declaredArity row =
  -- SELF-REFERENCE IS FENCED ON IDENTITY, NOT ON METADATA ABOUT IT. The first
  -- version of this gate compared SCC ids and skipped the comparison when the
  -- caller's id was empty, so `mod:f` calling `mod:f` with both SCC ids unset
  -- was admitted — as an EXTERNAL dependency, which is the most wrong possible
  -- reading of a direct self-call. The identity test needs no metadata to be
  -- present and cannot be evaded by omitting it.
  if eq targetId callerId
  then Just (RsnSccAssumptionNotValidated { sccId = concat "self-reference: " callerId })
  -- MUTUAL recursion needs the SCC ids, and an ABSENT id is UNDECIDED rather
  -- than "not in the same SCC". The first version skipped the comparison on an
  -- empty caller id so unrelated functions would not collide on the empty
  -- string — and that let `f -> g -> f` through whenever neither carried SCC
  -- metadata, which is precisely the case a caller controls by omission.
  -- Undecided fences: no caller SCC identity, no admission.
  else if eq callerSccId ""
  then Just (RsnSccAssumptionNotValidated { sccId = concat "no SCC identity for " callerId })
  else if eq row.sccId callerSccId
  then Just (RsnSccAssumptionNotValidated { sccId = callerSccId })
  else if not (eq row.outcome "Solved")
  then Just (RsnMissingEvidence { useId = targetId, observed = concat "the target's published scheme is not Solved: " row.outcome })
  else if gt (length row.diagnostics) 0
  then Just (RsnMissingEvidence { useId = targetId, observed = "the target's published scheme carries authored diagnostics" })
  else if not (eq row.quantified 0)
  -- Condition 2, and the reason constructor was minted for exactly this: the
  -- ground arrow this occurrence would need is the output of a per-use solve
  -- that has no representation to key itself by.
  then Just (RsnUnsolvedUseInstance { useId = targetId })
  else
    case vteEffectRefusal targetId row of
      Just reason -> Just reason
      Nothing -> vteArrowRefusal targetId declaredArity row.arrow

-- SC-EFF, AND `requires` ABSENCE IS NEGATIVE EVIDENCE ONLY. A ground arrow
-- proves nothing about purity: effects live outside the value type by design,
-- so `deletePage : (id: String) -> Unit` is perfectly ground and performs
-- state IO. Worse, an EMPTY `requires` does not prove purity either — a
-- bodyless declaration whose handler performs network IO carries no
-- `@requires` at all. So a Fenel-bodied target must have empty `requires`, and
-- a non-Fenel root must additionally carry a POSITIVE per-root attestation.
@label "Trusted entry effect refusal"
@description "The SC-eff refusal for a target: any declared `requires` fences, and a non-Fenel-bodied root additionally needs a positive purity/totality/honesty attestation, because `requires` absence never proves purity. Nothing when the target is admissible on the effect axis."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteEffectRefusal : (targetId: String) -> (row: VteSchemeRow) -> Maybe ValidationReason
vteEffectRefusal targetId row =
  if gt (length row.requires) 0
  then Just (RsnEffectfulScEff { requires = row.requires })
  else if eq row.origin "FenelBody"
  then Nothing
  else if not row.attestedPure
  then Just (RsnPrimitiveClaimMismatch { functionId = targetId, detail = "a non-Fenel root without a positive purity attestation" })
  else if not row.attestedTotal
  then Just (RsnPrimitiveClaimMismatch { functionId = targetId, detail = "a non-Fenel root without a positive totality attestation" })
  else if not row.attestedHonest
  then Just (RsnPrimitiveClaimMismatch { functionId = targetId, detail = "a non-Fenel root without a positive signature-honesty attestation" })
  else Nothing

@label "Trusted entry scheme for target"
@description "The published scheme row for a target id, or Nothing. AMBIGUITY IS NOTHING, NOT THE FIRST MATCH: two rows for one target is a table this entry cannot read, and taking the first would make the answer depend on table order. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteSchemeFor : (schemes: [VteSchemeRow]) -> (targetId: String) -> Maybe VteSchemeRow
vteSchemeFor schemes targetId =
  if not (eq (vteSchemeCount schemes targetId 0) 1)
  then Nothing
  else vteSchemeFirst schemes targetId

@label "Trusted entry scheme count"
@description "How many published scheme rows name one target. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteSchemeCount : (schemes: [VteSchemeRow]) -> (targetId: String) -> (acc: Nat) -> Nat
vteSchemeCount schemes targetId acc =
  case head schemes of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just row ->
      let next = if eq row.targetId targetId then add acc 1 else acc
      vteSchemeCount (eufRest schemes) targetId next

@label "Trusted entry scheme first"
@description "The first published scheme row naming a target, called only after the count is exactly one. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteSchemeFirst : (schemes: [VteSchemeRow]) -> (targetId: String) -> Maybe VteSchemeRow
vteSchemeFirst schemes targetId =
  case head schemes of
    Nothing -> Nothing
    Just row ->
      if eq row.targetId targetId
      then Just row
      else vteSchemeFirst (eufRest schemes) targetId

-- ---- the authority (SC-sig) ----------------------------------------------

@label "Trusted entry authority for"
@description "The exact OccurrenceAuthority the certificate set supplies for a target, or the reason there is none. A certificate whose scheme identity disagrees with the published scheme is STALE, not merely different — admitting it would let a certificate verified against an older declaration authorize the current one."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteAuthorityFor : (certificates: [VteCertificateRow]) -> (row: VteSchemeRow) -> Result ValidationReason OccurrenceAuthority
vteAuthorityFor certificates row =
  if not (eq (vteCertificateCount certificates row.targetId 0) 1)
  then Err (RsnMissingPrimitiveCertificate { functionId = row.targetId })
  else
    case vteCertificateFirst certificates row.targetId of
      Nothing -> Err (RsnMissingPrimitiveCertificate { functionId = row.targetId })
      Just cert ->
        if not (vcaHashEq cert.schemeHash row.schemeHash)
        then Err (RsnStaleEvidence { useId = row.targetId, observed = "the certificate's scheme identity is not the published scheme identity" })
        else if eq cert.kind "dependency"
        then Ok (OsaExternalDependency { depRef = DependencyCertificateRef { functionId = row.targetId, dependencyCertificateHash = cert.certificateHash } })
        else if eq cert.kind "primitive"
        then Ok (OsaVerifiedPrimitive { rootRef = VerifiedPrimitiveCertificateRef { functionId = row.targetId, schemeHash = row.schemeHash, implementationHash = cert.implementationHash, implementationOrigin = row.origin, authorityHash = cert.certificateHash } })
        else Err (RsnMissingPrimitiveCertificate { functionId = concat row.targetId (concat " kind=" cert.kind) })

@label "Trusted entry certificate count"
@description "How many certificate rows name one target. More than one is an ambiguous certificate set and refuses. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteCertificateCount : (certificates: [VteCertificateRow]) -> (targetId: String) -> (acc: Nat) -> Nat
vteCertificateCount certificates targetId acc =
  case head certificates of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just cert ->
      let next = if eq cert.targetId targetId then add acc 1 else acc
      vteCertificateCount (eufRest certificates) targetId next

@label "Trusted entry certificate first"
@description "The first certificate row naming a target, called only after the count is exactly one. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteCertificateFirst : (certificates: [VteCertificateRow]) -> (targetId: String) -> Maybe VteCertificateRow
vteCertificateFirst certificates targetId =
  case head certificates of
    Nothing -> Nothing
    Just cert ->
      if eq cert.targetId targetId
      then Just cert
      else vteCertificateFirst (eufRest certificates) targetId

-- ---- the carrier row join ------------------------------------------------

@label "Trusted entry row for identity"
@description "The ledger row of the carrier node that ISSUED one occurrence identity. Reached by walking the bundled children — never by a path lookup, because a path lookup is exactly the sibling-occurrence hazard the annotated carrier exists to remove."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteRowFor : (node: VcaNode) -> (useId: VcaUseId) -> Maybe UseLedgerRow
vteRowFor node useId =
  case node.identity of
    VciUse { useId = here } ->
      if vcaUseIdEq here useId
      then head node.rows
      else vteRowForKids node.children useId
    _ -> vteRowForKids node.children useId

@label "Trusted entry row for identity in children"
@description "Fold the identity-to-row search over a bundled child list. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteRowForKids : (children: [VcaChild]) -> (useId: VcaUseId) -> Maybe UseLedgerRow
vteRowForKids children useId =
  case head children of
    Nothing -> Nothing
    Just c ->
      case vteRowFor c.node useId of
        Just row -> Just row
        Nothing -> vteRowForKids (eufRest children) useId

-- ---- the branded preflight -----------------------------------------------

@label "Trusted entry preflight"
@description "The AUTHORITATIVE preflight. Beyond the bijection and snapshot checks a caller-supplied table can be given, it re-derives every entry's binding digest and refuses a mismatch, so an entry cannot be moved, re-targeted or re-valued; and it binds the whole admission under an outer digest covering the caller, the snapshot, the eligibility and the entry-digest vector as a table root."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vtePreflight : (request: VteRequest) -> (entries: [VteEntry]) -> (classes: [VteClassified]) -> VteOutcome
vtePreflight request entries classes =
  case vteRequestRefusal request of
    Just reason -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
    Nothing ->
  -- THIS FUNCTION TAKES NO CARRIER, AND THAT IS THE SECOND REPAIR IT HAS NEEDED.
  --
  -- Round 1: it took the eligibility VERDICT as an argument, so a direct call
  -- with a fenced carrier, empty identities, empty entries and a caller-supplied
  -- `EligEvidenceComplete` satisfied every check it performed (all vacuous on
  -- empty lists) and admitted having run no gate at all.
  --
  -- Round 2 showed the first repair was not enough. Requiring the carrier to
  -- carry `request.callerId` and `vteInputsHash request` BINDS it, and binding
  -- is not provenance: the caller holds the request, so it can compute both and
  -- still hand over a carrier whose `identities`, `root` and `readiness` it
  -- invented. Re-deriving readiness from that carrier's own rows only proves the
  -- forgery is self-consistent. **A forged carrier plus a matching forged entry
  -- list would still have admitted.**
  --
  -- So the carrier is not an argument at all: it is REBUILT from the request by
  -- the same `vcaCarrier` call `vteValidate` makes. On the honest path that is
  -- the identical carrier; on any other path the rebuilt one is the true one and
  -- the supplied entries have to match ITS identities. Everything downstream —
  -- the projection walk, the readiness gate, the fence check, the eligibility
  -- verdict — then runs against material this function derived rather than
  -- received.
  case vteCallerRefusal request.callerId (length request.paramNames) request.callerScheme of
    Just reason -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
    Nothing ->
      let inputsHash = vteInputsHash request
      let claimed = request.provenance
      let bodyDigest = validationCanonicalDigest (vteBodyDomain 0) request.body
      let provenance = LedgerBodyProvenance { bodyHash = bodyDigest.hex, astAbi = claimed.astAbi, parser = claimed.parser }
      case vcaCarrier request.callerId request.body request.paramNames request.routes request.arities provenance request.flatRoutes request.wireProjections request.ctorFields inputsHash of
        VcoFenced { reason = reason } -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
        VcoCarrier { carrier = rebuilt } -> vteCheckedPreflight request rebuilt entries classes

@label "Trusted entry checked preflight"
@description "The preflight over a carrier already proven to belong to this request, with eligibility RECOMPUTED rather than accepted. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteCheckedPreflight : (request: VteRequest) -> (carrier: VcaCarrier) -> (entries: [VteEntry]) -> (classes: [VteClassified]) -> VteOutcome
vteCheckedPreflight request carrier entries classes =
  case vteFirstUnsupported carrier.root of
    Just bad -> VtaRefused { reason = RsnUnsupportedSyntax { form = vteProjectionForm bad.projection, path = bad.path } }
    Nothing ->
      case vteReadinessRefusal carrier of
        Just reason -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
        Nothing ->
          if gt (length carrier.fences) 0
          then VtaRefused { reason = RsnCheckerAbstained { detail = "the carrier recorded at least one fenced occurrence" } }
          else if vcaIdentitiesCollide carrier.identities
          then VtaRefused { reason = RsnUnsupportedSyntax { form = "carrier-duplicate-occurrence-identity", path = [] } }
          else
            let eligibility = vteEligible request carrier.identities
            case eligibility of
              EligUnsupported { reason = reason } -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
              EligEvidenceIncomplete { reason = reason } -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
              EligEvidenceComplete -> vteAssemble request carrier eligibility entries classes

@label "Trusted entry assemble"
@description "Bind the minted table to the carrier and to itself: bijection with the live identities, one shared snapshot, every entry digest re-derived, and every instance re-projected from its target's own published arrow. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteAssemble : (request: VteRequest) -> (carrier: VcaCarrier) -> (eligibility: ValidationEligibility) -> (entries: [VteEntry]) -> (classes: [VteClassified]) -> VteOutcome
vteAssemble request carrier eligibility entries classes =
  -- THE BIJECTION BECAME A PARTITION CORRESPONDENCE, and the partition is
  -- RE-DERIVED here rather than accepted. `vteClassCounts` recomputes every
  -- identity's class from THIS carrier's own rows; a supplied table that
  -- classified a function identity as Γ-class in order to omit its entry —
  -- exactly the forged-carrier attack this module's history documents — fails
  -- the count comparison and then fails `vteClassesAgree`, which checks each
  -- classified row's class against the re-derivation identity by identity.
  let counts = vteClassCounts carrier.identities 0 0
  if not (eq (length entries) counts.entryBearing)
  then VtaRefused { reason = RsnMissingEvidence { useId = carrier.callerId, observed = "the minted table and the carrier's entry-bearing identities are not in bijection" } }
  else if not (eq (length classes) counts.classified)
  then VtaRefused { reason = RsnMissingEvidence { useId = carrier.callerId, observed = "the classified rows and the carrier's non-entry-bearing identities are not in bijection" } }
  else if not (vteEntriesAreEntryBearing entries)
  then VtaRefused { reason = RsnStaleEvidence { useId = carrier.callerId, observed = "a minted entry names an identity whose re-derived class is not entry-bearing" } }
  else if not (vteClassesAgree classes)
  then VtaRefused { reason = RsnStaleEvidence { useId = carrier.callerId, observed = "a classified row's class is not the class its own identity re-derives to" } }
  else if not (vteCoverageOk carrier entries classes)
  then VtaRefused { reason = RsnMissingEvidence { useId = carrier.callerId, observed = "a carrier identity is covered by zero or more than one admission row" } }
  -- THE STRONGEST CHECK, AND THE ONE A SUPPLIED TABLE CANNOT PASS: every entry
  -- is RE-MINTED from the rebuilt carrier and the request, and its digest must
  -- equal the supplied one. The digest covers every semantic field — arity,
  -- origin, profile, saturation, locality, enters-target, authority, scheme and
  -- instance — so this proves the entry IS this mint's output rather than
  -- merely self-consistent. Without it a caller could supply `origin =
  -- "FenelBody"` or a favourable `declaredArity`, recompute the digest over its
  -- own claim, and unlock a rule the real target does not qualify for.
  else if not (vteEntriesRemint request carrier entries)
  then VtaRefused { reason = RsnStaleEvidence { useId = carrier.callerId, observed = "a minted entry does not re-mint to itself from this request and carrier" } }
  else if not (vteEntriesShareSnapshot entries carrier.snapshot)
  then VtaRefused { reason = RsnStaleEvidence { useId = carrier.callerId, observed = "a minted entry does not carry this carrier's snapshot" } }
  else if not (vteEntriesShareProfile entries request.executorProfile)
  then VtaRefused { reason = RsnStaleEvidence { useId = carrier.callerId, observed = "a minted entry does not carry this request's executor profile" } }
  else if not (vteDigestsVerify carrier entries)
  then VtaRefused { reason = RsnStaleEvidence { useId = carrier.callerId, observed = "a minted entry's binding digest does not re-derive from its own contents" } }
  else if not (vteInstancesDerive request entries)
  then VtaRefused { reason = RsnStaleEvidence { useId = carrier.callerId, observed = "a minted entry's instance is not the projection of its target's published arrow at its declared arity" } }
  else
    -- 7. THE CONSTRUCT-AND-CONSUME SEAM (D6). The pre-checker classifier runs
    --    FIRST — it is the thing that must discharge the eta-stage demands
    --    readiness let through, and it refuses closed when it cannot. Only then
    --    does the arm run, inside this same closed computation, over material
    --    this function derived rather than received.
    --
    --    THE VERDICT IS COMPUTED BEFORE THE DIGEST AND COVERED BY IT. An
    --    admission digest that stopped at the table root would authenticate the
    --    evidence and leave the verdict free-floating, so the same authentic
    --    admission could be re-presented beside a forged `VchPassed`. Only the
    --    verdict's closed TAG enters the payload — a counterexample is a
    --    `Maybe Any` and canonicalising an arbitrary host value into a digest is
    --    the hazard this module already refuses elsewhere.
    --
    --    AND `VtaAdmitted` IS NOT A MEMBERSHIP CLAIM. It says the evidence is
    --    trusted and complete; the verdict says what that evidence decided.
    --    Judgment-1 may reach `VdValidated` only from a `VchPassed`, and the
    --    field is REQUIRED so no consumer can read the first without the second.
    case vchClassify entries carrier of
      Just reason -> VtaRefused { reason = reason }
      Nothing ->
        let verdict = vchValidateFunction entries carrier request.callerScheme request.paramNames
        let admission = VteAdmission {
              callerId = carrier.callerId,
              snapshot = carrier.snapshot,
              eligibility = eligibility,
              entries = entries,
              classes = classes,
              digest = vteAdmissionDigest request carrier eligibility entries classes verdict
            }
        VtaAdmitted { admission = admission, verdict = verdict }

@label "Trusted entry coverage"
@description "Every live carrier identity is covered EXACTLY ONCE by the admission — an entry-bearing one by exactly one entry and no classified row, a Γ-class or rule-class one by exactly one classified row and no entry. A count comparison alone cannot see this: two entries naming one identity and none naming another satisfy every length check while dropping an occurrence."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteCoverageOk : (carrier: VcaCarrier) -> (entries: [VteEntry]) -> (classes: [VteClassified]) -> Boolean
vteCoverageOk carrier entries classes = vteCoverageGo carrier.identities entries classes

@label "Trusted entry coverage worker"
@description "Walk the carrier identities, requiring each to be named exactly once by the table its re-derived class assigns and zero times by the other. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteCoverageGo : (identities: [VcaUseId]) -> (entries: [VteEntry]) -> (classes: [VteClassified]) -> Boolean
vteCoverageGo identities entries classes =
  case head identities of
    Nothing -> true
    Just useId ->
      let inEntries = vteEntryCountFor useId entries 0
      let inClasses = vteClassCountFor useId classes 0
      case vteClassOf useId of
        Nothing -> false
        Just class ->
          case class of
            VtcEntryBearing ->
              if not (and (eq inEntries 1) (eq inClasses 0))
              then false
              else vteCoverageGo (eufRest identities) entries classes
            _ ->
              if not (and (eq inEntries 0) (eq inClasses 1))
              then false
              else vteCoverageGo (eufRest identities) entries classes

@label "Trusted entry entry count for identity"
@description "How many minted entries name one occurrence identity. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteEntryCountFor : (useId: VcaUseId) -> (entries: [VteEntry]) -> (acc: Nat) -> Nat
vteEntryCountFor useId entries acc =
  case head entries of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just entry ->
      let next = if vcaUseIdEq entry.useId useId then add acc 1 else acc
      vteEntryCountFor useId (eufRest entries) next

@label "Trusted entry class count for identity"
@description "How many classified rows name one occurrence identity. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteClassCountFor : (useId: VcaUseId) -> (classes: [VteClassified]) -> (acc: Nat) -> Nat
vteClassCountFor useId classes acc =
  case head classes of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just row ->
      let next = if vcaUseIdEq row.useId useId then add acc 1 else acc
      vteClassCountFor useId (eufRest classes) next

@label "Trusted entry entries re-mint"
@description "Every supplied entry RE-MINTS to itself from this request and this rebuilt carrier — the mint is re-run for the entry's own occurrence identity and the resulting binding digest must equal the supplied one. Re-deriving a digest from a supplied entry proves consistency; re-minting proves provenance."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteEntriesRemint : (request: VteRequest) -> (carrier: VcaCarrier) -> (entries: [VteEntry]) -> Boolean
vteEntriesRemint request carrier entries =
  case head entries of
    Nothing -> true
    Just entry ->
      case vteMintOne request carrier entry.useId of
        Err reason -> false
        Ok fresh ->
          if not (vcaHashEq fresh.digest entry.digest)
          then false
          else vteEntriesRemint request carrier (eufRest entries)

@label "Trusted entry class counts"
@description "Re-derive how many of a carrier's live identities are entry-bearing and how many fall in the Γ and rule classes, from the identities themselves. An unclassifiable identity counts in NEITHER bucket, so a carrier carrying one can satisfy no correspondence and refuses. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteClassCounts : (identities: [VcaUseId]) -> (entryBearing: Nat) -> (classified: Nat) -> a
vteClassCounts identities entryBearing classified =
  case head identities of
    Nothing -> { entryBearing = entryBearing, classified = classified }
    Just useId ->
      case vteClassOf useId of
        Nothing -> vteClassCounts (eufRest identities) entryBearing classified
        Just class ->
          case class of
            VtcEntryBearing -> vteClassCounts (eufRest identities) (add entryBearing 1) classified
            _ -> vteClassCounts (eufRest identities) entryBearing (add classified 1)

@label "Trusted entry entries are entry-bearing"
@description "Every minted entry names an identity whose class RE-DERIVES to entry-bearing. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteEntriesAreEntryBearing : (entries: [VteEntry]) -> Boolean
vteEntriesAreEntryBearing entries =
  case head entries of
    Nothing -> true
    Just entry ->
      case vteClassOf entry.useId of
        Nothing -> false
        Just class ->
          case class of
            VtcEntryBearing -> vteEntriesAreEntryBearing (eufRest entries)
            _ -> false

@label "Trusted entry classes agree"
@description "Every classified row carries exactly the class its own occurrence identity re-derives to, and none of them re-derives to entry-bearing. This is the forgery check: a supplied classification that disagrees with the derivation is refused rather than believed. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteClassesAgree : (classes: [VteClassified]) -> Boolean
vteClassesAgree classes =
  case head classes of
    Nothing -> true
    Just row ->
      case vteClassOf row.useId of
        Nothing -> false
        Just derived ->
          if not (eq (vteClassKey derived) (vteClassKey row.class))
          then false
          else
            case derived of
              VtcEntryBearing -> false
              _ -> vteClassesAgree (eufRest classes)

@label "Trusted entry entries share profile"
@description "Every minted entry carries exactly this request's executor profile. Without it an entry minted under one profile could be re-presented inside an admission taken under another, and the per-entry digest would still verify against its own stored field. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteEntriesShareProfile : (entries: [VteEntry]) -> (profile: VteExecutorProfile) -> Boolean
vteEntriesShareProfile entries profile =
  case head entries of
    Nothing -> true
    Just entry ->
      if not (eq (vteExecutorProfileKey entry.executorProfile) (vteExecutorProfileKey profile))
      then false
      else vteEntriesShareProfile (eufRest entries) profile

@label "Trusted entry entries share snapshot"
@description "Every minted entry carries exactly this carrier's snapshot. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteEntriesShareSnapshot : (entries: [VteEntry]) -> (snapshot: VcaSnapshot) -> Boolean
vteEntriesShareSnapshot entries snapshot =
  case head entries of
    Nothing -> true
    Just entry ->
      if not (vcaSnapshotEq entry.snapshot snapshot)
      then false
      else vteEntriesShareSnapshot (eufRest entries) snapshot

@label "Trusted entry digests verify"
@description "Every minted entry's digest re-derives from the entry's own contents. Re-deriving rather than trusting is the point: this is the check a caller-supplied table could never pass by construction, because a supplied entry would have to carry the digest of material it did not produce."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteDigestsVerify : (carrier: VcaCarrier) -> (entries: [VteEntry]) -> Boolean
vteDigestsVerify carrier entries =
  case head entries of
    Nothing -> true
    Just entry ->
      let expected = vteEntryDigestOf carrier.snapshot entry
      if not (vcaHashEq expected entry.digest)
      then false
      else vteDigestsVerify carrier (eufRest entries)

-- THE INSTANCE IS RE-DERIVED, NOT HASHED, and that is the stronger check. A
-- digest over the instance proves only that the arrow was not edited after it
-- was minted; re-projecting the target's published arrow proves it is the arrow
-- that target actually declares. The digest still carries the instance's closed
-- tag, so the two checks fail independently rather than through one channel.
@label "Trusted entry instances derive"
@description "Every minted entry's instance is exactly the projection of its target's own published arrow. This is what a caller-supplied table can never satisfy by construction: it would have to carry an arrow it derived from the same scheme row this entry resolves independently."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteInstancesDerive : (request: VteRequest) -> (entries: [VteEntry]) -> Boolean
vteInstancesDerive request entries =
  case head entries of
    Nothing -> true
    Just entry ->
      let useId = entry.useId
      case vteSchemeFor request.schemes useId.targetId of
        Nothing -> false
        Just row ->
          if not (vcaHashEq row.schemeHash entry.schemeHash)
          then false
          -- THE DECLARED ARITY IS RE-RESOLVED, NOT READ OFF THE ENTRY, and it
          -- has to be: the arity SELECTS the projection (the zero-formal
          -- wrapper), so an entry that carried a different arity would re-derive
          -- a different instance and this check would compare the forgery
          -- against itself.
          else
            case vteArityFor request.arities useId.targetId of
              Nothing -> false
              Just declaredArity ->
                if not (eq declaredArity entry.declaredArity)
                then false
                else if not (eq row.origin entry.origin)
                then false
                else
                  let expected = vteInstanceArrow declaredArity row.arrow
                  if not (eq expected entry.instance)
                  then false
                  else vteInstancesDerive request (eufRest entries)

-- ---- the digests ---------------------------------------------------------

-- THE PAYLOAD IS LABELLED, and every field that can change the MEANING of the
-- entry is in it. A digest over (useId, snapshot, schemeHash, instance) alone
-- binds mutation but not meaning: a run with a matching dependency certificate
-- and a run with none share that tuple exactly and mean `SueAuthorized` versus
-- `NsuNoCertificate`. The resolved occurrence fields are in here for the same
-- reason — `VcaUseId` omits saturation, locality and enters-target, and an
-- arity-table change flips all three without touching the key.
@label "Trusted entry occurrence digest"
@description "The binding digest for one occurrence: its identity, its resolved occurrence fields, the carrier snapshot, the scheme identity, the derived instance AND the exact authority. Everything that can change what the entry MEANS, not merely what it points at."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteEntryDigest : (snapshot: VcaSnapshot) -> (useId: VcaUseId) -> (scheme: VteSchemeRow) -> (row: UseLedgerRow) -> (locality: UseLocality) -> (authority: OccurrenceAuthority) -> (instance: SetExpr) -> (declaredArity: Nat) -> (profile: VteExecutorProfile) -> ValidationHash
vteEntryDigest snapshot useId scheme row locality authority instance declaredArity profile =
  validationCanonicalDigest (vteOccurrenceDomain 0) {
    version = vteEntryVersion 0,
    callerId = useId.callerId,
    role = useId.role,
    path = vteSegKeys useId.path [],
    targetId = useId.targetId,
    kind = useId.kind,
    saturation = vteSaturationKey row.saturation,
    locality = vteLocalityKey locality,
    entersTarget = row.entersTarget,
    snapshotBody = snapshot.bodyHash,
    snapshotAstAbi = snapshot.astAbi,
    snapshotParser = snapshot.parser,
    snapshotCarrier = snapshot.carrierVersion,
    snapshotInputs = snapshot.inputsHash.hex,
    schemeDomain = scheme.schemeHash.domain,
    schemeHex = scheme.schemeHash.hex,
    authority = vteAuthorityKey authority,
    instance = vteSetKey instance,
    declaredArity = declaredArity,
    origin = scheme.origin,
    executorProfile = vteExecutorProfileKey profile
  }

@label "Trusted entry occurrence digest of entry"
@description "Re-derive a minted entry's digest from the entry itself, so the preflight compares a derivation against a stored value rather than a value against itself. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteEntryDigestOf : (snapshot: VcaSnapshot) -> (entry: VteEntry) -> ValidationHash
vteEntryDigestOf snapshot entry =
  let useId = entry.useId
  validationCanonicalDigest (vteOccurrenceDomain 0) {
    version = vteEntryVersion 0,
    callerId = useId.callerId,
    role = useId.role,
    path = vteSegKeys useId.path [],
    targetId = useId.targetId,
    kind = useId.kind,
    saturation = vteSaturationKey entry.saturation,
    locality = vteLocalityKey entry.locality,
    entersTarget = entry.entersTarget,
    snapshotBody = snapshot.bodyHash,
    snapshotAstAbi = snapshot.astAbi,
    snapshotParser = snapshot.parser,
    snapshotCarrier = snapshot.carrierVersion,
    snapshotInputs = snapshot.inputsHash.hex,
    schemeDomain = entry.schemeHash.domain,
    schemeHex = entry.schemeHash.hex,
    authority = vteAuthorityKey entry.authority,
    instance = vteSetKey entry.instance,
    declaredArity = entry.declaredArity,
    origin = entry.origin,
    executorProfile = vteExecutorProfileKey entry.executorProfile
  }

@label "Trusted entry admission digest"
@description "The outer digest binding the WHOLE admission: the caller, the snapshot, the eligibility verdict, the certificate set, the registry generation and the entry-digest vector as a table root. It exists because an entry-level digest cannot see that a table with a certificate and a table without one mean opposite things."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteAdmissionDigest : (request: VteRequest) -> (carrier: VcaCarrier) -> (eligibility: ValidationEligibility) -> (entries: [VteEntry]) -> (classes: [VteClassified]) -> (verdict: VchVerdict) -> ValidationHash
vteAdmissionDigest request carrier eligibility entries classes verdict =
  let snapshot = carrier.snapshot
  validationCanonicalDigest (vteAdmissionDomain 0) {
    version = vteEntryVersion 0,
    callerId = carrier.callerId,
    snapshotBody = snapshot.bodyHash,
    snapshotAstAbi = snapshot.astAbi,
    snapshotParser = snapshot.parser,
    snapshotCarrier = snapshot.carrierVersion,
    snapshotInputs = snapshot.inputsHash.hex,
    registryGeneration = request.registryGeneration,
    validatorVersion = request.validatorVersion,
    eligibility = vteEligibilityKey eligibility,
    readiness = vteReadinessKey carrier.readiness,
    certificates = vteCertificateKeys request.certificates [],
    executorProfile = vteExecutorProfileKey request.executorProfile,
    classes = vteClassKeys classes [],
    verdict = vchVerdictKey verdict,
    tableRoot = vteEntryDigestKeys entries []
  }

-- THE INPUTS DIGEST BINDS THE COMBINATION, not the tables one by one. Five
-- sibling digests would let a caller pair a route table frozen from one
-- registry generation with a scheme table from another and still verify every
-- one of them; a single digest over a labelled payload cannot be split that
-- way. `paramNames` and `arities` are in here because both change the
-- carrier's ANSWERS — the first decides lexical-versus-registered resolution,
-- the second decides saturation and enters-target — and neither was bound
-- before.
@label "Trusted entry inputs digest"
@description "The combined digest of every table the carrier's answers depend on: param names, frozen routes, flat routes, arities, wire projections, published schemes, certificates, the registry generation and the validator version. One digest over a labelled payload, so the COMBINATION is bound and not merely each table."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteInputsHash : (request: VteRequest) -> ValidationHash
vteInputsHash request =
  validationCanonicalDigest (vteInputsDomain 0) {
    version = vteEntryVersion 0,
    carrierVersion = vcaCarrierVersion 0,
    callerId = request.callerId,
    paramNames = request.paramNames,
    routes = vteRouteKeys request.routes [],
    flatRoutes = vteRouteKeys request.flatRoutes [],
    arities = vteArityKeys request.arities [],
    wires = vteWireKeys request.wireProjections [],
    ctorFields = vteCtorFieldKeys request.ctorFields [],
    schemes = vteSchemeKeys request.schemes [],
    certificates = vteCertificateKeys request.certificates [],
    registryGeneration = request.registryGeneration,
    validatorVersion = request.validatorVersion,
    executorProfile = vteExecutorProfileKey request.executorProfile
  }

-- ---- digest key projections ----------------------------------------------
--
-- EVERY PROJECTION BELOW IS TOTAL AND STRUCTURED. Nothing renders a number:
-- a cloned decider context BOXES host numbers, so a key built by `toString`
-- would render the host object and silently collide. Ordinals ride as their own
-- labelled numeric fields and the canonical encoder handles them.

@label "Trusted entry segment keys"
@description "The typed path as a list of structured segment keys — tag, ordinal and name, the same total triple the carrier's own segment equality is decided on. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteSegKeys : (path: [UsePathSeg]) -> (acc: a) -> a
vteSegKeys path acc =
  case head path of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just seg ->
      let key = { tag = vcaSegKind seg, index = vcaSegIndex seg, name = vcaSegName seg }
      vteSegKeys (eufRest path) (appendItem key acc)

@label "Trusted entry saturation key"
@description "The structured key of a use saturation — the closed tag plus its supplied count. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteSaturationKey : (saturation: UseSaturation) -> a
vteSaturationKey saturation =
  case saturation of
    VusExact -> { tag = "VusExact", supplied = 0 }
    VusUnder { supplied = supplied } -> { tag = "VusUnder", supplied = supplied }
    VusOver { supplied = supplied } -> { tag = "VusOver", supplied = supplied }

@label "Trusted entry locality key"
@description "The closed tag of a use locality. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteLocalityKey : (locality: UseLocality) -> String
vteLocalityKey locality =
  case locality of
    VulExternal -> "VulExternal"
    VulCurrentScc -> "VulCurrentScc"
    VulLexical -> "VulLexical"

@label "Trusted entry authority key"
@description "The structured key of one occurrence authority — its closed tag plus the exact certificate identity it rests on. The certificate hash is IN the key: an entry whose authority came from a different certificate is a different entry."
@tags "engine" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
vteAuthorityKey : (authority: OccurrenceAuthority) -> a
vteAuthorityKey authority =
  case authority of
    OsaExternalDependency { depRef = depRef } ->
      { tag = "OsaExternalDependency", functionId = depRef.functionId, hash = depRef.dependencyCertificateHash.hex, extra = "" }
    OsaVerifiedPrimitive { rootRef = rootRef } ->
      { tag = "OsaVerifiedPrimitive", functionId = rootRef.functionId, hash = rootRef.authorityHash.hex, extra = rootRef.implementationOrigin }
    OsaCurrentSccAssumption { sccId = sccId, targetFunctionId = targetFunctionId, schemeHash = schemeHash } ->
      { tag = "OsaCurrentSccAssumption", functionId = targetFunctionId, hash = schemeHash.hex, extra = sccId }
    OsaLexicalBinding { binderId = binderId, monotypeHash = monotypeHash } ->
      { tag = "OsaLexicalBinding", functionId = binderId, hash = monotypeHash.hex, extra = "" }
    OsaLexicalRegisteredCallable { binderId = binderId, originUseId = originUseId, targetFunctionId = targetFunctionId, residualArrowHash = residualArrowHash } ->
      { tag = "OsaLexicalRegisteredCallable", functionId = targetFunctionId, hash = residualArrowHash.hex, extra = concat binderId (concat "/" originUseId) }
    OsaConstructorRule { ownerIdentity = ownerIdentity, targetId = targetId, schemeHash = schemeHash, ruleVersion = ruleVersion, wireProjectionHash = wireProjectionHash } ->
      { tag = "OsaConstructorRule", functionId = targetId, hash = schemeHash.hex, extra = concat ownerIdentity (concat "/" (concat ruleVersion (concat "/" wireProjectionHash.hex))) }
    OsaIntrinsicRule { ruleId = ruleId, ruleVersion = ruleVersion } ->
      { tag = "OsaIntrinsicRule", functionId = ruleId, hash = "", extra = ruleVersion }

@label "Trusted entry eligibility key"
@description "The structured key of an eligibility verdict. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteEligibilityKey : (eligibility: ValidationEligibility) -> String
vteEligibilityKey eligibility =
  case eligibility of
    EligEvidenceComplete -> "EligEvidenceComplete"
    EligEvidenceIncomplete -> "EligEvidenceIncomplete"
    EligUnsupported -> "EligUnsupported"

@label "Trusted entry readiness key"
@description "The structured key of a whole-ledger readiness judgment, including the pending count so an admission cannot be moved between two readiness states. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteReadinessKey : (readiness: VckReadiness) -> a
vteReadinessKey readiness =
  case readiness of
    VrdStructurallyReady -> { tag = "VrdStructurallyReady", pending = 0 }
    VrdNeedsRefinement { pending = pending } -> { tag = "VrdNeedsRefinement", pending = length pending }
    VrdFinalFence -> { tag = "VrdFinalFence", pending = 0 }

@label "Trusted entry route keys"
@description "One route table as an ordered list of structured keys. ORDER IS PRESERVED, not sorted: two tables differing only in order resolve identically today, and a digest that normalized the order would stop being able to say they were different inputs. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteRouteKeys : (routes: [ValidationRoute]) -> (acc: a) -> a
vteRouteKeys routes acc =
  case head routes of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just route ->
      let key = { name = route.name, status = route.status, targetId = route.targetId, kind = route.kind, candidates = route.candidates }
      vteRouteKeys (eufRest routes) (appendItem key acc)

@label "Trusted entry arity keys"
@description "The arity table as an ordered list of structured keys. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteArityKeys : (arities: [ValidationArity]) -> (acc: a) -> a
vteArityKeys arities acc =
  case head arities of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just entry ->
      vteArityKeys (eufRest arities) (appendItem { targetId = entry.targetId, arity = entry.arity } acc)

@label "Trusted entry wire keys"
@description "The constructor wire-projection table as an ordered list of structured keys. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteWireKeys : (wires: [ValidationWireProjection]) -> (acc: a) -> a
vteWireKeys wires acc =
  case head wires of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just w -> vteWireKeys (eufRest wires) (appendItem { source = w.source, wire = w.wire } acc)

@label "Trusted entry constructor field keys"
@description "The constructor field-layout table as an ordered list of structured keys, carrying each field's semantic-key DIAGNOSTIC projection and its wire name. The diagnostic projection is used because a FieldKey is a constructor value the canonical encoder would render structurally anyway, and the pair (diagnostic, wire) already distinguishes two field vectors that differ. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteCtorFieldKeys : (rows: [ValidationCtorFieldRow]) -> (acc: a) -> a
vteCtorFieldKeys rows acc =
  case head rows of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just row ->
      let key = { source = row.source, fields = vteCtorFieldVector row.fields [] }
      vteCtorFieldKeys (eufRest rows) (appendItem key acc)

@label "Trusted entry constructor field vector"
@description "One constructor's field vector as ordered structured keys. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteCtorFieldVector : (fields: [VcaCtorField]) -> (acc: a) -> a
vteCtorFieldVector fields acc =
  case head fields of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just f -> vteCtorFieldVector (eufRest fields) (appendItem { semanticKey = fieldKeyDiagnostic f.semanticKey, wireName = f.wireName } acc)

@label "Trusted entry scheme keys"
@description "The published scheme table as an ordered list of structured keys, including the attestation bits and the requires vector — everything the admission conditions read. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteSchemeKeys : (schemes: [VteSchemeRow]) -> (acc: a) -> a
vteSchemeKeys schemes acc =
  case head schemes of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just row ->
      let key = {
            targetId = row.targetId,
            schemeDomain = row.schemeHash.domain,
            schemeHex = row.schemeHash.hex,
            quantified = row.quantified,
            diagnostics = row.diagnostics,
            outcome = row.outcome,
            requires = row.requires,
            origin = row.origin,
            attestedPure = row.attestedPure,
            attestedTotal = row.attestedTotal,
            attestedHonest = row.attestedHonest,
            sccId = row.sccId
          }
      vteSchemeKeys (eufRest schemes) (appendItem key acc)

@label "Trusted entry certificate keys"
@description "The certificate set as an ordered list of structured keys. It rides in BOTH the inputs digest and the admission digest: the first binds which certificates were offered, the second binds which admission they produced. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteCertificateKeys : (certificates: [VteCertificateRow]) -> (acc: a) -> a
vteCertificateKeys certificates acc =
  case head certificates of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just cert ->
      let key = {
            targetId = cert.targetId,
            kind = cert.kind,
            certificateDomain = cert.certificateHash.domain,
            certificateHex = cert.certificateHash.hex,
            schemeDomain = cert.schemeHash.domain,
            schemeHex = cert.schemeHash.hex,
            implementationHex = cert.implementationHash.hex,
            ruleVersion = cert.ruleVersion
          }
      vteCertificateKeys (eufRest certificates) (appendItem key acc)

@label "Trusted entry digest keys"
@description "The entry-digest vector, in mint order — the admission's TABLE ROOT. Internal helper."
@tags "engine" "internal" "signature-substrate" "phase-4" "validation" "entry"
@category "engine-internal"
vteEntryDigestKeys : (entries: [VteEntry]) -> (acc: a) -> a
vteEntryDigestKeys entries acc =
  case head entries of
    Nothing -> acc
    Just entry ->
      let d = entry.digest
      vteEntryDigestKeys (eufRest entries) (appendItem { domain = d.domain, hex = d.hex } acc)
